WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.500
Chapter 10: Multi-Agent Collaboration.

2
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:17.500
The first nine chapters focused on a single Agent: first building its context, knowledge, tools, and interaction capabilities, then using evaluation, post-training, and continual evolution to improve it over time.

3
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:25.125
This chapter advances the question from “How do we build and improve one Agent?” to “How do we organize multiple Agents?

4
00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:33.512
so that division of labor, communication, and mutual verification can tackle tasks that are difficult for one Agent to handle alone.

5
00:00:33.512 --> 00:00:48.687
OpenAI once proposed a five-level scale of AI capabilities: Level 1, Conversationalists; Level 2, Reasoners; Level 3, Agents; Level 4, Innovators; and Level 5, Organizations.

6
00:00:48.687 --> 00:00:53.787
Multi-agent collaboration is often presented as one path to Level 5.

7
00:00:53.787 --> 00:01:03.600
Here, however, "Organizations" denotes a capability level—AI that can do the work of an entire organization—rather than an architectural requirement.

8
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.012
A sufficiently powerful single Agent could, in principle, reach it as well.

9
00:01:09.012 --> 00:01:17.037
In today's engineering reality, however, a single Agent remains constrained by its model's capabilities and context window.

10
00:01:17.037 --> 00:01:24.587
Getting multiple Agents to work together is about far more than letting specialists with different expertise "cover each other's gaps.

11
00:01:24.587 --> 00:01:30.587
The more fundamental point is this: the intelligence of a group can exceed that of any individual.

12
00:01:30.587 --> 00:01:46.525
Human civilization is the proof—one person's intellect is limited, yet through division of labor, collaboration, debate, and the accumulation of knowledge across generations, human society as a whole exhibits intelligence far beyond any single genius.

13
00:01:46.525 --> 00:01:59.062
Agent groups may give rise to the same kind of collective intelligence: even if each Agent is only as capable as a human expert, a well-organized group could surpass the combined capabilities of all human experts.

14
00:01:59.062 --> 00:02:22.437
In From AGI to ASI, Google DeepMind lists "large-scale multi-agent collectives" as a key pathway toward superintelligence (ASI)—just as human general intelligence aggregates into societies and organizations that transcend individuals, the collective intelligence of many AGI-level Agents working together may exhibit cognitive capabilities far beyond the simple sum of its members.

15
00:02:22.437 --> 00:02:36.687
Multi-agent collaboration, then, is not merely an engineering workaround for a single model's context window and capability limits—it may be a fundamental path from "expert-level AI" toward "surpassing humanity as a whole.

16
00:02:36.687 --> 00:02:40.900
A Classification Framework for Multi-Agent Collaboration.

17
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:49.075
Building a multi-agent system starts with two core design dimensions, which together determine its basic architecture and implementation.

18
00:02:49.075 --> 00:02:53.225
Dimension 1: Shared vs. Non-Shared Context.

19
00:02:53.225 --> 00:03:00.262
This is the most fundamental architectural decision, determining how information is passed between multiple Agents.

20
00:03:00.262 --> 00:03:09.750
Shared context means that a subsequent Agent receives the complete conversation history and trajectory (as defined in Chapter 1) of the preceding Agent.

21
00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:23.325
When the system prompt and tool set change at each stage, the system treats the new stage as a different Agent because its identity, responsibilities, and capabilities have changed, even though it retains all the memory of its predecessor.

22
00:03:23.325 --> 00:03:34.625
For example, after a requirements analyst writes a requirements document, the developer receives not only the document but also the full record of communication between the analyst and the user.

23
00:03:34.625 --> 00:03:39.412
The developer assumes a new role while retaining all prior context.

24
00:03:39.412 --> 00:03:45.937
The advantage is that no information is lost; each Agent can review details from any previous stage.

25
00:03:45.937 --> 00:03:49.725
The challenge is that the context can expand rapidly.

26
00:03:49.876 --> 00:03:59.288
Non-shared context means that each Agent maintains an independent context and conversation history and cannot directly access the other Agents' work traces.

27
00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:11.176
This is like collaboration between different departments: everyone works independently at their own desk, exchanging information through shared documents and meeting minutes rather than constantly watching each other's screens.

28
00:04:11.176 --> 00:04:19.651
This model offers better modularity and isolation; each Agent only needs to focus on information relevant to its own responsibilities.

29
00:04:19.651 --> 00:04:30.988
The system is also easier to extend and maintain—adding a new Agent does not require modifying the internal logic of existing Agents, only defining interfaces and data formats.

30
00:04:30.988 --> 00:04:37.763
Since Agents do not share context, information must be passed through explicit communication mechanisms.

31
00:04:37.763 --> 00:04:57.113
Classic distributed systems settled this question long ago: operating-systems textbooks tell us that inter-process communication (IPC) ultimately comes in just two paradigms—shared memory (one side writes and the other reads the same block of storage) and message passing (data is explicitly sent to the other side).

32
00:04:57.113 --> 00:05:02.451
Communication mechanisms between Agents fall within these same two paradigms.

33
00:05:02.451 --> 00:05:04.926
There are three common methods:

34
00:05:04.926 --> 00:05:16.451
Tool call parameters: Wrap the downstream Agent as a tool, then pass structured data through its parameters; this is suitable for scenarios requiring well-typed, clearly structured data.

35
00:05:16.451 --> 00:05:30.163
Shared file system: Agents exchange information by reading and writing intermediate artifacts (documents, code, etc.) in a shared directory, suitable for scenarios with large artifacts or where persistence is needed.

36
00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:35.776
Message bus: A dedicated intermediary that passes messages between Agents.

37
00:05:35.776 --> 00:05:42.451
Agents do not call each other directly but send messages to the bus, which forwards them to the target Agent.

38
00:05:42.451 --> 00:05:53.738
Mapped onto the two IPC paradigms, the shared file system corresponds to "shared memory," while tool call parameters and the message bus are forms of "message passing.

39
00:05:53.738 --> 00:06:01.201
Tool parameters are delivered synchronously with a call; messages on a bus are delivered asynchronously through an intermediary.

40
00:06:01.201 --> 00:06:04.088
Each paradigm has its trade-offs.

41
00:06:04.088 --> 00:06:11.813
Go has a widely quoted maxim: "Do not communicate by sharing memory; instead, share memory by communicating.

42
00:06:11.813 --> 00:06:17.926
As illustrated in Figure 10-1: Shared Context vs. Non-Shared Context.

43
00:06:17.926 --> 00:06:21.501
Dimension 2: Collaboration Topology.

44
00:06:21.501 --> 00:06:28.738
The second dimension is collaboration topology: the structure through which control and information flow among Agents.

45
00:06:28.738 --> 00:06:31.601
There are three typical topologies:

46
00:06:31.601 --> 00:06:49.588
Peer Collaboration Pattern: A small number of Agents (typically 2-3) interact as equals, forming an iterative improvement loop—like writing a paper where one person drafts it and another annotates and revises it, with the quality after several rounds far exceeding what one person could achieve alone.

47
00:06:49.588 --> 00:07:04.213
Manager Pattern (Orchestration Pattern): A centralized Manager Agent is responsible for task planning and scheduling, while multiple sub-agents each handle specific subtasks—like a project manager leading several specialized engineers on a project.

48
00:07:04.213 --> 00:07:12.463
Decentralized Pattern: There is no runtime central controller; Agents communicate with each other like humans to collaborate on tasks.

49
00:07:12.463 --> 00:07:15.463
Terminology: Graph Engineering.

50
00:07:15.463 --> 00:07:36.913
The term "Graph Engineering," which became popular in July 2026, generally refers in today's Agent context to explicitly designing an execution graph: nodes are Agents, ordinary programs, or human decisions; edges define task dependencies, conditional routing, and failure paths; and structured state flows between nodes.

51
00:07:36.913 --> 00:07:49.076
The "collaboration topology" discussed in this chapter is the multi-agent subset of that idea—peer collaboration, manager orchestration, and decentralized handoffs are different graph topologies.

52
00:07:49.076 --> 00:08:04.026
Because the name is still new and is easily confused with knowledge graphs, GraphRAG, and execution traces, this book continues to use the more stable terms "collaboration topology" and "orchestration" as its primary vocabulary.

53
00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:11.005
The detailed design and applicable scenarios for each pattern will be discussed in dedicated subsections later.

54
00:08:10.955 --> 00:08:14.605
When Is Multi-Agent Truly Better Than a Single Agent?

55
00:08:14.605 --> 00:08:24.255
Before diving into specific collaboration architectures, let's answer a more fundamental question: When are multiple Agents truly needed, and when is one enough?

56
00:08:24.255 --> 00:08:29.442
The answer will serve as a reference point for every engineering approach that follows.

57
00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:41.330
A series of recent studies converges on a clear framework—and the core criterion is a single question: Does the collaboration provide information that a single Agent could not obtain while producing its answer?

58
00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:51.467
Table 10-1 shows which collaboration modes introduce new information and helps assess whether multi-agent collaboration offers substantive value over a single Agent.

59
00:08:51.467 --> 00:08:57.142
Table 10-1 Information Gain Comparison of Multi-Agent Collaboration Modes

60
00:08:57.142 --> 00:09:08.080
Collaboration Mode: Self-review by the same model (re-reading its own output); Introduces New Information?: No; Effect: Usually ineffective or even harmful.

61
00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:18.305
Collaboration Mode: Different Agents debating the same text; Introduces New Information?: No; Effect: Comparable to a single Agent with equal compute.

62
00:09:18.305 --> 00:09:29.867
Collaboration Mode: Reviewer uses test execution results to review code; Introduces New Information?: Yes (execution feedback); Effect: Significant improvement.

63
00:09:29.867 --> 00:09:42.430
Collaboration Mode: Reviewer uses rendered screenshots to review frontend/PPT code; Introduces New Information?: Yes (visual feedback); Effect: Significant improvement.

64
00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:53.405
Collaboration Mode: Reviewer uses external tools to verify facts; Introduces New Information?: Yes (tool feedback); Effect: Significant improvement.

65
00:09:53.405 --> 00:10:08.905
The 2025 RLEF paper (Reinforcement Learning from Execution Feedback) found that training a model via reinforcement learning to use code-execution feedback for iterative improvement significantly outperformed independently sampling the model multiple times.

66
00:10:08.905 --> 00:10:20.480
The key is that each iteration introduces real execution results (compilation errors, test failures, runtime exceptions)—information that did not exist when the model wrote the code.

67
00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:39.317
For webpage-generation tasks, the 2025 WebGen-Agent study reported that multi-level visual feedback, combining screenshots with vision-language-model descriptions, improved Claude 3.5 Sonnet's benchmark performance from 26.4% to 51.9%, nearly doubling it.

68
00:10:39.317 --> 00:10:50.330
This framework helps resolve an apparent contradiction: some academic studies find that a single Agent is sufficient, while multi-agent systems often perform better in engineering practice.

69
00:10:50.330 --> 00:11:03.567
The studies often test multiple Agents that inspect and discuss the same text, as in debate, whereas effective engineering systems commonly add external feedback from code execution, visual rendering, or tools.

70
00:11:03.567 --> 00:11:06.817
Only the latter introduces new information.

71
00:11:06.817 --> 00:11:17.355
Nearly all effective uses of the three architectures discussed later—peer collaboration, orchestration, and decentralization—can be understood through this criterion.

72
00:11:17.355 --> 00:11:23.180
Anthropic's 2026 vulnerability-discovery experiment provides one example.

73
00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:32.242
Forty-five Agents coordinated their searches through a shared forum, reviewed one another's findings, and submitted results to a separate arbiter Agent.

74
00:11:32.242 --> 00:11:44.230
The coordinated swarm found 266 vulnerabilities using 27 million tokens, while independently parallel Agents found only 21 using 6.5 million tokens.

75
00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:56.767
In an open search space, communication lets a multi-agent system shift its attention dynamically and develop specializations, trading a larger token budget for broader coverage and more varied discovery paths.

76
00:11:56.932 --> 00:11:59.932
Step Budget and Agent Performance.

77
00:11:59.882 --> 00:12:08.244
A related question is how an Agent's step budget—the number of tool calls or iteration rounds it may use—affects performance.

78
00:12:08.244 --> 00:12:20.394
More steps might seem certain to help: with 30 steps, an Agent may have time only to implement core functionality, whereas 300 steps allow it to plan, implement, test, and refine.

79
00:12:20.394 --> 00:12:32.619
However, the 2025 Google paper Budget-Aware Tool-Use Enables Effective Agent Scaling reached a counterintuitive conclusion: simply giving an Agent more steps does not guarantee better performance.

80
00:12:32.619 --> 00:12:41.219
Standard Agents lack "budget awareness"; even with 300 steps, they tend to conduct shallow searches and quickly reach a plateau.

81
00:12:41.219 --> 00:12:51.482
To use additional steps effectively, Agents need a mechanism that adapts their strategy to the remaining resources, exploring broadly at first and narrowing their focus later.

82
00:12:51.482 --> 00:13:05.244
The 2026 BAVT (Budget-Aware Value Tree Search) approach further introduced step-level value evaluation, adjusting the balance between exploration and exploitation according to the proportion of the budget remaining.

83
00:13:05.244 --> 00:13:11.319
As the budget decreases, the Agent shifts from broad exploration to deeper investigation.

84
00:13:11.319 --> 00:13:16.057
These findings have direct implications for multi-agent system design.

85
00:13:16.057 --> 00:13:24.169
For example, in the orchestration pattern, the Manager Agent should not simply distribute tasks to sub-agents and wait for results.

86
00:13:24.169 --> 00:13:34.707
Instead, it should dynamically allocate step budgets based on task complexity—simple subtasks get fewer steps; complex subtasks get ample steps.

87
00:13:34.707 --> 00:13:44.532
It should also guide sub-agents to use these budgets wisely (plan first, then implement, then test, then improve), rather than diving straight in.

88
00:13:44.532 --> 00:13:49.407
One more consideration must come before any design decision: cost.

89
00:13:49.407 --> 00:14:04.719
Parallel exploration and iterative refinement cost money—Anthropic has disclosed that its multi-agent research system consumes about 15 times the tokens of a normal conversation, and that token usage alone explains about 80% of the performance difference.

90
00:14:04.719 --> 00:14:17.844
The gains from a multi-agent system must therefore be large enough to justify costs that may be several times, or even an order of magnitude, higher; otherwise, a well-tuned single Agent is usually the better bargain.

91
00:14:17.844 --> 00:14:21.557
Multi-Agent Collaboration with Shared Context.

92
00:14:21.557 --> 00:14:38.294
In multi-agent collaboration with shared context, each stage is an independent Agent (with its own system prompt and tool set), but it inherits the complete trajectory of the preceding Agent—much like a colleague taking over a shift who can leaf through every work log the predecessor left behind.

93
00:14:38.294 --> 00:14:47.007
The core advantage of this inheritance-based collaboration is zero information loss: every Agent can review details from any previous stage.

94
00:14:47.007 --> 00:14:54.294
The challenge is keeping the current Agent focused on its own responsibilities rather than distracted by the mass of inherited history.

95
00:14:54.294 --> 00:15:00.882
In complex tasks, an Agent's role and responsibilities may change significantly across stages.

96
00:15:00.882 --> 00:15:08.807
If a single static system prompt is used throughout, it will either be too general or become an unwieldy collection of instructions.

97
00:15:08.807 --> 00:15:17.507
Multi-stage role switching changes the system prompt and tool set according to the current stage, allowing the Agent to work in the most appropriate role.

98
00:15:17.507 --> 00:15:24.457
The key architectural choice is whether role guidance is carried by a replacement system prompt or by a loaded Skill.

99
00:15:24.457 --> 00:15:30.482
The former can enforce a hard tool boundary, but changes the request prefix at every switch.

100
00:15:30.482 --> 00:15:46.419
The latter keeps the static prefix stable and appends SKILL.md to the trajectory, which is usually friendlier to KV/prompt caching; a Skill remains behavioral guidance, so sensitive or side-effectful tools still require a code-enforced Harness policy gate.

101
00:15:46.588 --> 00:16:09.038
Choice: transfer_to_agent; Role guidance: Replace the system prompt and usually the tool set; Tool visibility: Only the current role's tools; Context/KV-cache effect: Each switch changes the request prefix and usually invalidates caching from that point; Constraint strength: Strong: out-of-scope tools can be absent from the schema.

102
00:16:08.988 --> 00:16:32.875
Choice: Skill; Role guidance: Keep a Skill directory in the fixed prompt and append SKILL.md on demand; Tool visibility: Usually the full catalog, or a stable search entry point; Context/KV-cache effect: The static prefix stays stable; Skill text is appended to the trajectory; Constraint strength: Weak: a Skill is an instruction, not a permission boundary.

103
00:16:32.875 --> 00:16:53.625
When the role difference comes mainly from knowledge, procedure, and writing style, prefer a Skill; when it involves permissions, tool isolation, compliance boundaries, or a class of actions that must be forbidden at run time, use an independent Agent or the transfer_to_agent tool, and restrict the tool calls with code at the Harness layer.

104
00:16:53.625 --> 00:17:01.975
Experiment 10-1 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Shared-context role switching—system prompt versus Skill

105
00:17:01.975 --> 00:17:08.850
Both paths use the same model, task, tools, role guidance and complete shared trajectory.

106
00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:20.838
The task is to find China's 2021–2023 new-energy vehicle sales, calculate CAGR, and write a Chinese investor summary of no more than 120 characters.

107
00:17:20.838 --> 00:17:23.888
Path 1: system-prompt switching.

108
00:17:23.888 --> 00:17:34.675
Five roles—triage, research, coding, data_analysis and writing—each expose only their dedicated tools plus transfer_to_agent.

109
00:17:34.675 --> 00:17:41.088
A handoff saves history, loads the target prompt and tool set, and resumes execution.

110
00:17:41.088 --> 00:17:43.425
Path 2: Skill.

111
00:17:43.425 --> 00:17:47.513
The system prompt and full tool catalog remain fixed.

112
00:17:47.513 --> 00:17:55.188
The model calls load_skill(name) and receives the same role document as a tool result in the shared trajectory.

113
00:17:55.188 --> 00:18:01.300
The static prefix remains unchanged, but hard permissions are enforced by Harness rules.

114
00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:06.600
The two paths should perform the same retrieval, calculation and length check.

115
00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:14.975
They differ in the carrier of role guidance and in the resulting tool boundary; a smoke trace alone cannot establish which path is superior.

116
00:18:14.975 --> 00:18:18.863
Multi-Agent Collaboration Without Shared Context.

117
00:18:18.863 --> 00:18:27.350
In an architecture without shared context, each Agent operates as an independent entity with its own context, trajectory, and state.

118
00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:44.188
Agents cannot directly access one another's internal context; collaboration relies entirely on explicit, structured data transfers through the three communication mechanisms introduced at the beginning of this chapter: tool call parameters, a shared file system, and a message bus.

119
00:18:44.188 --> 00:18:50.388
Earlier in this chapter, we compared communication between Agents to inter-process communication.

120
00:18:50.388 --> 00:18:55.488
We can extend this analogy to other parts of the system (Table 10-2

121
00:18:55.488 --> 00:19:01.200
Table 10-2 Correspondence Between Multi-Agent Systems and Operating Systems

122
00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:10.538
Operating System: Program (executable file); Multi-Agent System: Static prefix (system prompt + tool definitions).

123
00:19:10.538 --> 00:19:16.125
Operating System: Process memory; Multi-Agent System: Trajectory.

124
00:19:16.125 --> 00:19:21.313
Operating System: CPU; Multi-Agent System: LLM.

125
00:19:21.313 --> 00:19:26.538
Operating System: Kernel; Multi-Agent System: Agent runtime.

126
00:19:26.538 --> 00:19:32.050
Operating System: System call; Multi-Agent System: Tool call.

127
00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:39.525
Operating System: fork (create child process); Multi-Agent System: spawn_subagent.

128
00:19:39.525 --> 00:19:46.463
Operating System: kill (send signal); Multi-Agent System: cancel_subagent.

129
00:19:46.463 --> 00:19:53.500
Operating System: ps (list processes); Multi-Agent System: list_agents.

130
00:19:53.500 --> 00:20:00.700
Operating System: Exit code and wait(); Multi-Agent System: Structured summary returned by the sub-agent.

131
00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:09.175
Operating System: Shared memory / message passing; Multi-Agent System: Shared file system / message passing.

132
00:20:09.175 --> 00:20:19.863
This abstraction is nothing new: private state, asynchronous messages, and the ability to create new members are precisely the basic setup of the 1970s Actor model.

133
00:20:19.863 --> 00:20:30.850
A multi-agent system can therefore be viewed as an LLM-based version of the Actor model, and much of the accumulated knowledge from operating systems and distributed systems applies directly.

134
00:20:31.012 --> 00:20:46.899
This process-style isolation brings several practical engineering benefits: each Agent can be developed and tested independently, new capabilities can be added without touching existing code, and multiple Agents can execute concurrently without contention over shared context.

135
00:20:46.849 --> 00:20:50.887
However, not sharing context also has costs.

136
00:20:50.887 --> 00:20:58.562
The most obvious is the information synchronization problem: how do Agents maintain a consistent understanding of the task state?

137
00:20:58.562 --> 00:21:02.374
Will information be lost or duplicated during transfer?

138
00:21:02.374 --> 00:21:11.937
Debugging also becomes more difficult—when problems arise, logs from multiple Agents must be reviewed to piece together the complete execution process.

139
00:21:11.937 --> 00:21:19.937
These issues make the design of interface specifications, data formats, and communication protocols critically important.

140
00:21:19.937 --> 00:21:26.487
Explicit collaboration without shared context relies on two topology-independent infrastructures.

141
00:21:26.487 --> 00:21:36.474
The first is the shared file system, the persistent medium through which Agents exchange artifacts with one another and with the user, forming the data plane of collaboration.

142
00:21:36.474 --> 00:21:49.274
The second is the communication and control mechanism, which supports message passing, status queries, execution termination, and resource scheduling between Agents, forming the control plane of collaboration.

143
00:21:49.274 --> 00:21:53.849
The three topologies below are all built on these two foundations.

144
00:21:53.849 --> 00:21:57.162
The File System from an Agent's Perspective.

145
00:21:57.162 --> 00:22:06.337
At the beginning of this chapter, the "shared file system" was listed as one of the three communication mechanisms for architectures without shared context.

146
00:22:06.337 --> 00:22:19.799
In a real system, the file system an Agent accesses is not a single storage system but a virtual file system in which storage systems with different sources, lifecycles, and permissions are mounted under one directory tree.

147
00:22:19.799 --> 00:22:36.899
The Agent accesses them through unified read_file/write_file/list_dir interfaces, while the underlying layers may be local temporary disks, persistent object storage, third-party cloud drive APIs, or read-only system resource packages.

148
00:22:36.899 --> 00:22:52.649
Clearly defining the composition of this directory tree—the visibility and lifecycle of each area—is a prerequisite for designing multi-agent collaboration: a significant portion of concurrency conflicts and information leaks stem from mixing areas that should be isolated.

149
00:22:52.649 --> 00:23:04.962
This directory tree amounts to the Agent's address space, and the four types of areas are memory segments with different permissions: some private and writable, some shared among multiple parties, and some read-only.

150
00:23:04.962 --> 00:23:12.462
The operating system's protection philosophy applies here as well: isolate by default and declare sharing explicitly.

151
00:23:12.462 --> 00:23:18.962
A mature multi-agent system typically organizes its file system into four types of storage area:

152
00:23:18.962 --> 00:23:22.662
I. Agent-Specific Workspace (Scratchpad).

153
00:23:22.662 --> 00:23:31.899
A private directory exclusive to each Agent instance, storing intermediate artifacts, temporary files, drafts, and debug logs.

154
00:23:31.899 --> 00:23:37.287
Its lifecycle is tied to the instance and is invisible to other Agents and users.

155
00:23:37.287 --> 00:23:54.387
Isolating the scratchpad serves two purposes: preventing temporary files from multiple Agents from overwriting each other, and keeping the main Agent's context lean—the trial-and-error process of sub-agents remains in their own workspace, with only the final artifact submitted to the shared space.

156
00:23:54.387 --> 00:24:02.124
This is the storage-level counterpart of Chapter 4's principle that sub-agents return structured summaries rather than full trajectories.

157
00:24:02.284 --> 00:24:05.596
II. Multi-Agent Shared Workspace.

158
00:24:05.546 --> 00:24:11.496
A collaboration area that multiple Agents can read and write, and that is visible to the user.

159
00:24:11.496 --> 00:24:26.884
It is the primary medium for exchanging artifacts between Agents in architectures without shared context: the Glossary Agent writes the term list, and the Translation Agent reads from it; users can also upload source files and download final deliverables here.

160
00:24:26.884 --> 00:24:31.684
Its lifecycle is tied to the entire task and requires persistence.

161
00:24:31.684 --> 00:24:46.234
As an area for concurrent reads and writes by multiple parties, it is a hotspot for concurrency conflicts—mechanisms such as optimistic locking and worktree isolation operate here, as detailed under "Failure Mode One" later in this chapter.

162
00:24:46.234 --> 00:24:56.659
Chapter 4's use of a volume mount at /workspace/shared to connect the main Agent, virtual computer, and virtual phone is a typical implementation of this layer.

163
00:24:56.659 --> 00:24:59.721
III. Mounted External Resources.

164
00:24:59.721 --> 00:25:14.696
Third-party information sources authorized by the user—Google Drive, Notion, Dropbox, enterprise wikis, etc.—are mapped to mount points in the file system (e.g., /mnt/gdrive) via adapters.

165
00:25:14.696 --> 00:25:22.009
An Agent accesses a Notion document by reading a file; the underlying adapter calls the corresponding API.

166
00:25:22.009 --> 00:25:49.984
Three characteristics distinguish this layer from local storage and must be explicitly handled during design: access is constrained by external permissions (the user's permissions in the source system determine the Agent's visibility), latency is higher and consistency is weaker (each read involves a network round trip, and external changes may not be immediately visible, so the data should be treated as eventually consistent), and access is primarily on-demand and read-only (writing back to

167
00:25:49.984 --> 00:25:56.584
external sources must be done cautiously, as erroneous writes could contaminate the user's real data).

168
00:25:56.584 --> 00:26:05.959
The unified file interface means the Agent does not need a custom tool for each data source, but it also masks these performance and security differences.

169
00:26:05.959 --> 00:26:14.134
Therefore, read-only/writable status, timeouts, and credential boundaries must be explicitly managed at the mount level.

170
00:26:14.134 --> 00:26:17.321
IV. Built-in System Resources.

171
00:26:17.321 --> 00:26:22.884
A resource package pre-installed by the system and shared read-only with all Agents.

172
00:26:22.884 --> 00:26:37.209
Typical examples are the Skills introduced in Chapters 2 and 4—knowledge documents and scripts organized as files, mounted at paths like /skills, accessed via progressive disclosure (index first, then expand on demand).

173
00:26:37.209 --> 00:26:43.684
Other examples include reference manuals, template libraries, and shared tool definitions.

174
00:26:43.684 --> 00:26:52.684
This layer is globally shared, read-only, stable across sessions, and can be read concurrently by all Agents without concurrency control.

175
00:26:52.684 --> 00:27:12.459
Figure 10-2 illustrates how these four area types are uniformly mounted under a single directory tree: the Agent accesses the entire tree through a unified interface, users upload and download files from the shared space, external data sources are mounted via adapters, and built-in system resources are provided read-only.

176
00:27:12.459 --> 00:27:19.796
As illustrated in Figure 10-2: Mounting structure of the four area types in the Agent Virtual File System.

177
00:27:19.796 --> 00:27:33.571
Table 10-3 compares these four area types across four dimensions—visibility, lifecycle, read/write permissions, and concurrency control—serving as a checklist for file system layout design.

178
00:27:33.571 --> 00:27:38.734
Table 10-3 Four area types of the Agent Virtual File System

179
00:27:38.734 --> 00:27:52.721
Area: Agent-Specific Workspace; Visibility: The owning Agent only; Lifecycle: Destroyed with the Agent instance; Read/Write: Read/Write; Concurrency Control: Not needed (private).

180
00:27:52.721 --> 00:28:09.221
Area: Multi-Agent Shared Workspace; Visibility: All collaborating Agents and the user; Lifecycle: Persists for the task duration; Read/Write: Read/Write; Concurrency Control: Required (optimistic lock / worktree).

181
00:28:09.221 --> 00:28:25.884
Area: Mounted External Resources; Visibility: Depends on external authorization; Lifecycle: Determined by the external source; Read/Write: Mostly read-only, writes require caution; Concurrency Control: Managed by the external source.

182
00:28:26.044 --> 00:28:39.406
Area: Built-in System Resources; Visibility: All Agents; Lifecycle: Stable across sessions; Read/Write: Read-only; Concurrency Control: Not needed (read-only).

183
00:28:39.356 --> 00:28:46.131
The value of the "file path as a universal interface" lies in treating a path as the unit of exchange.

184
00:28:46.131 --> 00:29:00.506
Whether Agents exchange artifacts, a main Agent hands input to a sub-agent, or organizations collaborate through A2A, they pass a lightweight path string rather than loading the file's contents into the context window (Chapter 4).

185
00:29:00.506 --> 00:29:11.294
This aligns with Chapter 5's concept of "the file system as the Agent's hub," which describes how a single Agent uses the file system to host memory and capabilities.

186
00:29:11.294 --> 00:29:23.919
Here, the same abstraction extends to multiple Agents: a virtual directory tree mounting private, shared, external, and built-in storage provides the storage foundation for multi-agent collaboration.

187
00:29:23.919 --> 00:29:27.281
Communication and Control Between Agents.

188
00:29:27.281 --> 00:29:35.119
While the file system solves the problem of artifact exchange between Agents, collaboration also requires a control plane.

189
00:29:35.119 --> 00:29:58.794
This is exactly where the lifecycle rows of Table 10-2 come into play: the tool primitives given in Chapter 4—creating (spawn_subagent), sending messages (send_message_to_subagent), canceling (cancel_subagent), and discovering (list_agents)—correspond to fork, message, kill, and ps in the process world.

190
00:29:58.794 --> 00:30:07.294
This section does not repeat the interface definitions but focuses on four often-overlooked capabilities essential for multi-agent collaboration.

191
00:30:07.294 --> 00:30:09.531
I. Message Passing.

192
00:30:09.531 --> 00:30:17.719
The simplest form is point-to-point: Agent A directly calls send_message_to_agent_b(content).

193
00:30:17.719 --> 00:30:28.031
This is suitable for scenarios with a fixed topology and a small number of Agents (e.g., the phone + computer dual-agent setup of Experiment 10-3 in this chapter).

194
00:30:28.031 --> 00:30:40.719
When the number of Agents increases and asynchronous parallelism is required, the number of point-to-point connections grows quadratically with the number of Agents, and both sender and receiver must be online simultaneously.

195
00:30:40.719 --> 00:30:55.194
In such cases, a message bus should be used (detailed later in this chapter under "Parallel Coordination Pattern"): Agents publish messages to the bus, which forwards them based on subscriptions, so the sender does not need to know the subscribers.

196
00:30:55.194 --> 00:31:12.656
Whether point-to-point or via a bus, messages should typically carry a structured envelope: sender ID, target (specific Agent or broadcast), message type (e.g., task_assigned/status_update/result/terminate), and a JSON payload.

197
00:31:12.656 --> 00:31:23.256
A unified envelope format ensures reliable routing and parsing by the receiver and makes the collaboration chain traceable—a key aspect of debugging multi-agent systems.

198
00:31:23.256 --> 00:31:25.756
II. Status Query.

199
00:31:25.756 --> 00:31:29.594
This is the most underestimated part of the control plane.

200
00:31:29.594 --> 00:31:41.281
Once a main Agent has dispatched a sub-agent, it needs visibility into the sub-agent's progress; otherwise, it can neither decide whether to keep waiting nor intervene when the sub-agent gets stuck.

201
00:31:41.281 --> 00:31:54.631
An intuitive approach is to borrow from RPC and define a get_subagent_status(agent_id) query interface that returns "running/completed/failed" plus a progress percentage.

202
00:31:54.631 --> 00:32:04.506
But such a pull interface turns out to be far less useful than expected: a sub-agent starts executing the moment it is created and runs until it completes or fails.

203
00:32:04.506 --> 00:32:15.819
It does not cycle through a series of queued states the way jobs in a traditional batch system do, just as Unix programming rarely needs to poll another process by its PID for running status.

204
00:32:15.819 --> 00:32:23.444
Polling also carries an inherent dilemma: poll too often and you waste tokens; poll too rarely and you react late.

205
00:32:23.444 --> 00:32:30.856
A more natural way to obtain status is to return to the two communication paradigms introduced at the beginning of this chapter.

206
00:32:31.012 --> 00:32:33.837
Getting status via message passing.

207
00:32:33.787 --> 00:32:38.337
The main Agent simply sends the sub-agent a message: "How's it going?

208
00:32:38.337 --> 00:32:41.724
The sub-agent replies at an opportune moment.

209
00:32:41.724 --> 00:32:58.124
Everything is asynchronous: sending the message does not block the main Agent's own execution, and when—or whether—the other side replies is a separate matter, just as a manager asks a subordinate for progress via instant messaging without requiring them to drop everything on the spot.

210
00:32:58.124 --> 00:33:14.349
Conversely, the sub-agent can also proactively send a message to report when it reaches a milestone; if the system already has a message bus, this is simply publishing a status_update to the bus (the "real-time monitoring" of Experiment 10-4 is this form).

211
00:33:14.349 --> 00:33:32.699
Whether status is requested explicitly or reported proactively, the status carried in the message should adopt a uniform state-machine vocabulary (executing, needs input, completed, failed)—the A2A protocol later in this chapter standardizes the task lifecycle into exactly such a set of states.

212
00:33:32.699 --> 00:33:36.012
Getting status via the shared file system.

213
00:33:36.012 --> 00:33:50.987
The most thorough form is trajectory persistence: as it executes, the sub-agent serializes each trajectory event to JSON and appends it to a filesystem log file—usually one file per session, one event per line, i.e., JSONL.

214
00:33:50.987 --> 00:33:59.862
The trajectory, defined in Chapter 1, is the complete sequence of user messages, model replies, tool calls, and results.

215
00:33:59.862 --> 00:34:15.199
The main Agent needs no status-reporting protocol; by reading this file directly, it can inspect the sub-agent's entire execution: which tool it is calling, what happened in its most recent step, and whether it is stuck in a loop of repeated failed retries.

216
00:34:15.199 --> 00:34:20.562
In process terms, this resembles reading another process's memory directly.

217
00:34:20.562 --> 00:34:28.837
It does not occupy the sub-agent's context, does not depend on its cooperation, and offers the finest observation granularity.

218
00:34:28.837 --> 00:34:34.649
But trajectory persistence should not be the main channel for passing information between Agents.

219
00:34:34.649 --> 00:34:43.999
A trajectory easily runs to tens of thousands of tokens, and the main Agent still has to distill it after reading—costly in both time and tokens.

220
00:34:43.999 --> 00:35:02.312
In most situations the more sensible choice is to agree on a progress file: when starting a sub-agent, the main Agent stipulates "write your progress to progress.md," the sub-agent updates that task list as it completes each item, and the main Agent can read this lightweight file at any time to see how things stand.

221
00:35:02.312 --> 00:35:12.649
This is equivalent to two processes carving out a small, agreed-format status region in shared memory: what is exposed is distilled progress, not the whole of memory.

222
00:35:12.649 --> 00:35:29.687
The progress file also enables stuck detection: if the last-modified time of progress.md (or the trajectory file) has not changed for more than N minutes, the sub-agent can be judged inactive and a timeout fallback triggered, so a blocked sub-agent does not drag the system down.

223
00:35:29.836 --> 00:35:32.711
III. Execution Termination.

224
00:35:32.661 --> 00:35:48.973
In parallel collaboration, a common scenario is "one succeeds, the rest become irrelevant"—multiple Agents search separately, and once one finds the target, the others should stop immediately (the cascading termination in Experiment 10-4 of this chapter).

225
00:35:48.973 --> 00:35:56.086
There are two levels of termination, and Unix users will recognize them as the distinction between SIGTERM and SIGKILL.

226
00:35:56.086 --> 00:36:12.936
Graceful termination is preferred: the main Agent sends a terminate signal, the sub-agent responds at a safe point in its current step, cleans up resources (closes browser sessions, writes pending files, releases locks), sends an acknowledgment (ack), and then exits.

227
00:36:12.936 --> 00:36:25.361
Forced termination is a fallback: directly terminating the process, used only when the sub-agent does not respond to the graceful signal, at the cost of potentially leaving dangling resources and incomplete writes.

228
00:36:25.361 --> 00:36:28.298
Two engineering points need attention.

229
00:36:28.298 --> 00:36:40.911
First, graceful termination requires the sub-agent to check periodically for the termination signal in its loop (similar to the interrupt mechanism in Chapter 6); otherwise, it cannot receive the signal.

230
00:36:40.911 --> 00:36:48.786
Second, cascading termination has a race condition: multiple sub-agents might report success nearly simultaneously.

231
00:36:48.786 --> 00:36:57.748
The main Agent must use a lock or idempotent design to ensure that only one success is accepted and that the termination signal is broadcast once.

232
00:36:57.748 --> 00:37:02.161
See the discussion of race conditions in Experiment 10-4.

233
00:37:02.161 --> 00:37:08.698
One loose end remains: after the main Agent terminates, what happens to sub-agents still running?

234
00:37:08.698 --> 00:37:22.711
The cleanest engineering approach borrows from Go's context—termination cascades down the creation relationship: cancel one Agent and all the sub-agents it spawned are canceled with it, preventing orphaned child Agents from being left behind.

235
00:37:22.711 --> 00:37:31.361
The "sub-agent checks for the termination signal at a safe point" above corresponds precisely to polling ctx.Done() in Go.

236
00:37:31.361 --> 00:37:46.223
Conversely, if you genuinely need a long-running background Agent detached from the main Agent (like Unix's nohup), let it start from a new lifecycle tree (corresponding to context.Background()), explicitly declaring that it does not terminate with its parent.

237
00:37:46.223 --> 00:37:49.598
IV. Resource Management and Scheduling.

238
00:37:49.598 --> 00:37:54.736
The other half of an operating system's job is allocating scarce resources.

239
00:37:54.736 --> 00:38:06.486
In the process world the scarce resources are CPU time and memory; in the Agent world they are tokens, money, and concurrency budget—every step a sub-agent takes consumes all three.

240
00:38:06.486 --> 00:38:30.311
This responsibility usually falls on the Manager or the runtime: set a step or token budget when starting a sub-agent, and stop once it is exceeded; give hard tasks to a strong model and mechanical tasks to a low-cost model; cap concurrency so that dozens of Agents don't exhaust the API quota at once; and when a more urgent task arrives, interrupt an executing sub-agent—this is preemption.

241
00:38:30.311 --> 00:38:37.048
Compared with a traditional operating system's scheduler, the manager Agent's notable advantage is that it can reason.

242
00:38:37.048 --> 00:38:51.673
A manager Agent can therefore launch several sub-agents to explore one problem in parallel and, based on their progress, decide which to give more resources and which to terminate for appearing to have gone astray—rather like an internal race within a company.

243
00:38:51.673 --> 00:38:58.373
Resource management and scheduling for multi-agent systems are still less mature than operating-system scheduling.

244
00:38:58.373 --> 00:39:05.198
Because they determine the system's maximum resource costs, they should be considered when designing the architecture.

245
00:39:05.198 --> 00:39:18.798
Artifact exchange (the data plane) together with message passing, status query, execution termination, and resource scheduling (the control plane) support a multi-Agent system without shared context.

246
00:39:18.798 --> 00:39:35.711
Based on the collaborative relationships among Agents and the characteristics of the control flow, collaboration without shared context divides into three main architectures—the peer collaboration pattern, the manager pattern, and the decentralized pattern—each suited to a different kind of task.

247
00:39:35.711 --> 00:39:40.461
Peer Collaboration Pattern: Mutual Checks and Iterative Improvement.

248
00:39:40.612 --> 00:39:48.137
Peer collaboration typically involves two or three Agents of equal standing giving one another feedback over multiple rounds.

249
00:39:48.087 --> 00:39:58.174
Its potential value lies in independent perspectives and cognitive diversity, but “multiple instances” do not necessarily produce “multiple ways of thinking.

250
00:39:58.174 --> 00:40:07.849
When the model, context, and scaffolding are highly similar, different Agents often make the same choices, turning local errors into systemic failures.

251
00:40:07.849 --> 00:40:20.224
Genuine diversity must be designed by varying models, contexts, tools, visible evidence, or responsibilities, and by having Agents judge independently before their results are aggregated.

252
00:40:20.224 --> 00:40:33.562
Compared to the manager and decentralized patterns, peer collaboration is far simpler to implement—define the two Agents' roles, the communication mechanism, and the iteration termination condition, and you have a running system.

253
00:40:33.562 --> 00:40:38.649
It is an ideal choice for quickly validating ideas and building prototypes.

254
00:40:38.649 --> 00:40:40.549
Loop Engineering.

255
00:40:40.549 --> 00:40:50.224
One of the most common uses of peer collaboration is to counter a frequent failure in Agent practice: premature termination—stopping with the job half done.

256
00:40:50.224 --> 00:41:02.849
It takes three typical forms; the examples below come from Coding Agents and from Pine AI, the Agent introduced in the Introduction that makes phone calls on users' behalf to deal with merchants and service providers.

257
00:41:02.849 --> 00:41:21.049
The first is claiming completion after only partial work: a Coding Agent writes the code, never runs the tests or tries the deployment, and reports "task complete"; a user gives Pine AI two errands, and it finishes the first, forgets the second, and cheerfully reports "all taken care of.

258
00:41:21.049 --> 00:41:38.974
The second is giving up too soon: declaring the whole job impossible after one blocked path—Pine AI can reach a merchant by phone, web form, or email, but after a single rejected call it tells the user "this can't be done," when switching channels and trying again would very likely have succeeded.

259
00:41:38.974 --> 00:41:58.287
The third is false success: the Agent believes the job is done, but the loop was never actually closed—the other side verbally agrees to a refund on the phone, yet the user still has to confirm a step in the mobile app; the Agent reports "all set," the user never learns there is a follow-up action, and the refund never lands.

260
00:41:58.287 --> 00:42:06.449
All three forms point to the same root cause: until it is verified, "done" is merely the model's claim, not proof.

261
00:42:06.449 --> 00:42:26.249
Turning claims into proofs is precisely the business of Loop Engineering, the last stage of Chapter 1's evolutionary arc: design a loop that keeps the Agent running—discover the next piece of work, execute, verify, record progress—and let a verifier, not the model itself, decide whether it is truly safe to stop.

262
00:42:26.249 --> 00:42:33.512
The human's role shifts accordingly from "the operator who prompts the Agent" to "the engineer who designs the loop.

263
00:42:33.512 --> 00:42:43.762
The term was coined in June 2026 by Addy Osmani; Boris Cherny, head of Claude Code at Anthropic, put it more bluntly: "I don't prompt Claude anymore.

264
00:42:43.762 --> 00:42:46.299
My job is to write loops.

265
00:42:46.299 --> 00:42:58.212
The central conclusion to emerge from that discussion was that the bottleneck of the loop is the verifier, not the model: with unreliable verification, a faster loop merely marks poor output as complete sooner.

266
00:42:58.212 --> 00:43:03.587
And as the Introduction says, practice comes first, naming comes later.

267
00:43:03.587 --> 00:43:13.837
Long before the term caught on, leading Agent teams—Pine AI among them—were already using "loop plus verification" against premature termination.

268
00:43:13.837 --> 00:43:19.662
The most effective way to organize that verification is the Proposer-Reviewer paradigm below.

269
00:43:19.662 --> 00:43:22.674
Concrete framework: LoopX.

270
00:43:22.674 --> 00:43:42.162
LoopX takes the loop out of the model's prompt and chat history and places it in a durable, agent-runtime-neutral control plane: the objective and boundary explain why the work exists; gates and todos determine what may happen now; evidence and quota determine whether it may continue; and handoffs let a later turn or another Agent resume it.

271
00:43:42.162 --> 00:43:46.462
It compresses one governed execution into a clear protocol:

272
00:43:46.462 --> 00:43:53.112
Code statement: LoopX decides → Agent executes → independent verifier proves → LoopX commits.

273
00:43:53.260 --> 00:43:58.935
The Agent still reasons, uses tools, and produces candidate artifacts.

274
00:43:58.885 --> 00:44:04.697
LoopX does not replace the Agent runtime; it governs continuity across turns.

275
00:44:04.697 --> 00:44:10.222
Only independently verified results may update durable progress and spend quota.

276
00:44:10.222 --> 00:44:19.085
Failed validation routes to repair or replanning, while human gates, wait states, and budget limits stop the loop before execution.

277
00:44:19.085 --> 00:44:28.372
This boundary turns a Loop Engineering principle into an inspectable system invariant: the model may propose “done,” but it cannot approve its own “done.

278
00:44:28.372 --> 00:44:42.610
LoopX v0.4.0 still labels the governed-Turn path experimental, so it is used here as a concrete framework for “loop + verification + stop conditions,” not as evidence of general task-quality uplift.

279
00:44:42.610 --> 00:44:46.122
Concrete framework: LongHorizon-Harness.

280
00:44:46.122 --> 00:44:53.785
LongHorizon-Harness and LoopX are both concrete implementations of Loop Engineering, but they point in different directions.

281
00:44:53.785 --> 00:45:10.547
LoopX targets a durable control plane for long-running Agent work; LongHorizon-Harness starts from multimodal Computer Use and tackles continuous execution when a single task spans a GUI, a CLI, several desktop applications, and repeated context refreshes.

282
00:45:10.547 --> 00:45:35.185
LongHorizon-Harness reframes long-horizon execution as task-state management and implements its loop as Manage–Execute–Audit (MEA): the Manager generates the next bounded subtask from the original objective, verified progress, failure evidence, and remaining work; the Executor changes the environment through the GUI or CLI in a fresh context; the Auditor then inspects the actual result read-only.

283
00:45:35.185 --> 00:45:43.047
Only what passes the audit enters the next round's task state, while failures are retained as the basis for recovery and replanning.

284
00:45:43.047 --> 00:45:52.472
Execution backends such as Claude Code and Codex CLI are reused through an adapter layer rather than by rewriting the Agent loop inside those backends.

285
00:45:52.472 --> 00:46:06.147
The value of this direction lies in separating task continuity from an ever-growing execution history: context may be refreshed and interface operations may fail, yet the next round still resumes from the most recently verified state.

286
00:46:06.147 --> 00:46:31.235
Holding the Qwen 3.7-Plus model and the Claude Code execution backend fixed and changing only the outer loop, the paper reports WeaveBench PassRate rising from 51.8% to 80.7%, OSWorld 2.0 binary completion from 2.8% to 8.3%, and Terminal-Bench 2.1 success from 69.7% to 77.2%.

287
00:46:31.235 --> 00:46:45.522
The cost is not fixed either: the first two benchmarks consumed 2.3× the baseline's total tokens and 3.6× its output tokens respectively, while token use on Terminal-Bench 2.1 fell by 24%.

288
00:46:45.522 --> 00:46:57.685
A real deployment must additionally handle state invalidated by a changing external environment or changing user requirements, and use round, time, and cost budgets to keep recovery loops from running forever.

289
00:46:57.844 --> 00:47:00.731
Public trajectories and reproduction.

290
00:47:00.681 --> 00:47:13.169
The project website publishes hundreds of run trajectories for WeaveBench, OSWorld 2.0, and Terminal-Bench 2.1, so the execution process and each role's records can be inspected directly.

291
00:47:13.169 --> 00:47:29.231
Take WeaveBench's WEB_task_16_webrtc_simulcast_layer_audit: the baseline trajectory and the MEA trajectory, both on the same Qwen 3.7-Plus model, can be compared side by side.

292
00:47:29.231 --> 00:47:44.169
The former got stuck on Wireshark interaction and retried repeatedly, scoring 0.59; the latter wrote failures and unmet evidence items back into task state so that later rounds handled only the gaps, scoring 0.92.

293
00:47:44.169 --> 00:47:57.431
This case shows “how a failure becomes the next round's input” and does not substitute for aggregate statistics; the environment, parameters, and launch scripts for the full experiments are in the pinned eval/ directory.

294
00:47:57.431 --> 00:47:59.981
Proposer-Reviewer Paradigm.

295
00:47:59.981 --> 00:48:04.781
As illustrated in Figure 10-3: Proposer-Reviewer Loop.

296
00:48:04.781 --> 00:48:09.131
Proposer-Reviewer is the canonical peer-collaboration paradigm.

297
00:48:09.131 --> 00:48:19.419
Chapter 5 already covered its design principles and practical applications in three experiments: PPT generation, video editing, and log visualization.

298
00:48:19.419 --> 00:48:30.969
The Proposer Agent generates code, while the Reviewer Agent renders the execution results, evaluates their quality using a vision-language model, and provides structured suggestions for improvement.

299
00:48:30.969 --> 00:48:34.994
The two iterate until the result meets the required standard.

300
00:48:34.994 --> 00:48:56.581
This paradigm is also applicable to scenarios like security review (Proposer generates an action plan, Reviewer checks compliance and potential risks), content moderation (Proposer drafts a reply, Reviewer checks business rules and language norms), and code review (Proposer writes code, Reviewer checks security and best practices).

301
00:48:56.581 --> 00:49:00.819
Why can't a single Agent generate and then review its own work?

302
00:49:00.819 --> 00:49:13.769
This is exactly where the criterion from "When Is Multi-Agent Truly Better Than a Single Agent?" earlier in this chapter applies—if the review does not introduce new information, it is just "asking the model to think again.

303
00:49:13.769 --> 00:49:17.019
Related research provides a clear answer.

304
00:49:17.019 --> 00:49:37.231
In their ICLR 2024 paper "Large Language Models Cannot Self-Correct Reasoning Yet," Huang et al. found that asking GPT-4 to review and correct its own answers without external feedback actually decreased accuracy—the model changed correct answers to incorrect ones more often than it changed incorrect answers to correct ones.

305
00:49:37.231 --> 00:49:46.931
The proposer-reviewer loop must satisfy a basic requirement: the reviewer examines independent evidence rather than merely restating the proposer's explanation.

306
00:49:46.931 --> 00:49:54.444
When returning work for revision, it must identify exactly what needs fixing and specify what the revision must achieve:

307
00:49:54.444 --> 00:49:59.181
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

308
00:49:59.181 --> 00:50:09.356
The reviewer must not be able to modify the tests, the evidence collector, or the release gate; otherwise "independent verification" degenerates into self-approval.

309
00:50:09.356 --> 00:50:16.606
A 2024 survey paper published in TACL, "When Can LLMs Actually Correct Their Own Mistakes?

310
00:50:16.606 --> 00:50:36.231
arXiv:2406.01297), further confirmed this conclusion: unless reliable external feedback is provided (e.g., test case execution results, verification output from external tools), relying solely on the model's own "self-correction" is largely ineffective.

311
00:50:36.231 --> 00:50:42.244
The CRITIC paper at ICLR 2024 provides an intuitive comparative experiment.

312
00:50:42.244 --> 00:50:52.219
CRITIC had the model use external tools (search engine, Python interpreter) to verify its own answers, leading to significant performance improvements.

313
00:50:52.219 --> 00:51:00.819
However, when the experimenters removed the tool verification step and only kept the model's self-assessment, most of the improvement disappeared.

314
00:51:00.819 --> 00:51:15.481
This indicates that the value of review lies not in "asking the model to think again," but in introducing new information that was not available during the model's generation—test results, rendered screenshots, compilation errors, external search results.

315
00:51:15.652 --> 00:51:25.339
Anthropic's 2026 experiment on long-running application development implemented this idea as a three-Agent planner–generator–evaluator architecture.

316
00:51:25.289 --> 00:51:29.927
The planner expanded a user's request into a product specification.

317
00:51:29.927 --> 00:51:42.477
The generator and evaluator first agreed on the completion criteria for each round; the generator then implemented the work, and the evaluator exercised the real application with Playwright and filed a defect report.

318
00:51:42.477 --> 00:51:45.614
Agents handed state off through files.

319
00:51:45.614 --> 00:51:57.752
The experiment suggests that when a task lies beyond what the current model can reliably complete alone, independent review grounded in external evidence can trade substantially higher cost for better development quality.

320
00:51:57.752 --> 00:51:59.564
Debate Pattern.

321
00:51:59.564 --> 00:52:05.864
Multiple Agents hold different positions, exploring the problem space through adversarial dialogue.

322
00:52:05.864 --> 00:52:18.202
For example, when evaluating a technical solution, Agent A plays the "supporter," listing the solution's advantages and opportunities, while Agent B plays the "opponent," pointing out risks and limitations.

323
00:52:18.202 --> 00:52:23.027
Each round of debate involves rebutting or extending the other's arguments.

324
00:52:23.027 --> 00:52:29.814
When a single Agent analyzes a problem, it often favors one perspective and overlooks counterevidence.

325
00:52:29.814 --> 00:52:37.014
Structured debate forces both positions to be developed fully, helping decision-makers reach a more balanced judgment.

326
00:52:37.014 --> 00:52:42.402
However, the practical effectiveness of debate remains contested in academia.

327
00:52:42.402 --> 00:52:55.864
A 2026 study by Tran and Kiela  compared a single Agent with five multi-agent architectures (sequential, debate, ensemble, parallel roles, subtask-parallel) on multi-hop reasoning tasks.

328
00:52:55.864 --> 00:53:07.652
They found that when the thinking-token budget was held constant, the single Agent performed on par with or even better than the multi-agent systems (unless context utilization was degraded to a certain point).

329
00:53:07.652 --> 00:53:24.189
The researchers provided an explanation based on the data processing inequality in information theory: multiple Agents in a debate process the exact same textual information, and each serial transmission of intermediate conclusions between Agents can only lose information, not create it.

330
00:53:24.189 --> 00:53:31.639
The benefits of the debate mode in some academic papers likely stem from multiple Agents consuming more total computation.

331
00:53:31.639 --> 00:54:00.952
It is important to clarify the boundary of this argument: it targets the information bottleneck caused by "multi-agent serial transmission of intermediate conclusions" and does not negate other approaches, such as multiple independent samples of the same problem followed by aggregation (e.g., self-consistency, majority voting), or leveraging the asymmetry in difficulty between generation and verification (writing an answer is hard, verifying it is easy) for a generation-verification division of

332
00:54:00.952 --> 00:54:02.364
labor.

333
00:54:02.364 --> 00:54:12.539
These scenarios either introduce additional independent sampling or exploit the asymmetric structure of the task itself, and are not within the scope of the data processing inequality.

334
00:54:12.539 --> 00:54:14.627
Brainstorming Pattern.

335
00:54:14.627 --> 00:54:21.252
Multiple Agents independently generate ideas, then share them with each other, inspiring one another.

336
00:54:21.252 --> 00:54:42.439
For example, in a product innovation task, Agent 1 proposes "adding social sharing features," Agent 2 is inspired to suggest "not just sharing to social networks, but also generating personalized sharing posters," and Agent 3 synthesizes the first two to propose "user-customizable poster templates forming a template marketplace.

337
00:54:42.439 --> 00:54:56.027
Different Agents have different "thinking preferences" (achieved through different prompts or models), and by stimulating each other, they explore a broader solution space to find creative combinations that a single Agent would struggle to conceive.

338
00:54:56.027 --> 00:54:58.352
Panel of Experts Pattern.

339
00:54:58.516 --> 00:55:06.178
Multiple Agents each represent the perspective of a specific professional domain, jointly discussing an interdisciplinary problem.

340
00:55:06.128 --> 00:55:24.616
For example, when evaluating the feasibility of a new product, an Engineer Agent analyzes the implementation difficulty from a technical standpoint, a Product Agent assesses market appeal from a user experience perspective, and an Operations Agent analyzes business viability from a cost and resource perspective.

341
00:55:24.616 --> 00:55:34.191
These Agents are not adversarial but complementary, together piecing together the full picture of the problem and identifying cross-domain constraints and opportunities.

342
00:55:34.191 --> 00:55:37.828
Manager Pattern: Centralized Coordination.

343
00:55:37.828 --> 00:55:49.378
When a task involves many subtasks, needs dynamic scheduling, or has complex dependencies among subtasks, peer collaboration is out of its depth, and the manager pattern is needed.

344
00:55:49.378 --> 00:56:09.191
The Manager Agent's job resembles that of a project manager: understand the overall task, break it into assignable subtasks, choose the right Agent for each, track progress, handle exceptions by retrying tasks, replacing Agents, or revising the plan, and finally integrate the Agents' outputs into the final result.

345
00:56:09.191 --> 00:56:16.553
From a system design perspective, the manager pattern models each specialized Agent as a tool that the Manager can invoke.

346
00:56:16.553 --> 00:56:26.341
The Manager's tool set includes not only traditional external tools, such as search and file operations, but also interfaces for invoking other Agents.

347
00:56:26.341 --> 00:56:36.216
The Manager invokes the appropriate Agent through a tool call, passes the task parameters and necessary context, waits for completion, and receives the result.

348
00:56:36.216 --> 00:56:45.566
From the Manager's perspective, calling an Agent is essentially no different from calling a regular tool: both involve sending a request and receiving a response.

349
00:56:45.566 --> 00:56:50.216
This unified abstraction makes the manager pattern easy to extend.

350
00:56:50.216 --> 00:56:58.728
Adding a capability requires only developing the corresponding Agent and registering it as a tool, without modifying the Manager's core logic.

351
00:56:58.728 --> 00:57:07.691
It also naturally supports heterogeneity: different Agents can use different models, prompts, tool sets, and even hardware environments.

352
00:57:07.691 --> 00:57:11.441
The manager pattern has inherent challenges, though.

353
00:57:11.441 --> 00:57:23.228
The Manager becomes the system's single-point bottleneck: it must understand the nature of every subtask, choose the right Agent, and pass context accurately; any misjudgment ripples through the whole flow.

354
00:57:23.228 --> 00:57:31.191
It must also maintain the global context of the entire task, which can balloon as the task deepens and Agent calls accumulate.

355
00:57:31.191 --> 00:57:40.328
The Manager therefore requires a carefully designed prompt, an effective context-management strategy, and appropriately granular task decomposition.

356
00:57:40.328 --> 00:57:51.953
The 2025 Plan-and-Act paper  provides an empirical analysis of this: in a Planner-Executor dual-agent architecture, a weak planner is the most critical bottleneck of the entire system.

357
00:57:51.953 --> 00:57:59.078
When the Planner's planning quality is high enough, good results can be achieved even with a relatively simple Executor.

358
00:57:59.078 --> 00:58:06.716
Conversely, if the Planner's task decomposition is wrong, all subsequent Executor work is built on a faulty premise.

359
00:58:06.716 --> 00:58:18.228
The study achieved a 57.58% success rate on the WebArena-Lite benchmark, and its core contribution was improving the Planner's planning ability, not the Executor's execution.

360
00:58:18.228 --> 00:58:27.828
The lesson: give the strongest model and the most carefully crafted prompt to the Manager (the planner), rather than spreading resources evenly across all Agents.

361
00:58:27.828 --> 00:58:36.128
A parallel manager must also define the settlement point as "the first verified success" rather than "the first claimed success"

362
00:58:36.128 --> 00:58:40.866
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

363
00:58:40.866 --> 00:58:52.503
settle_once must be idempotent (usually protected by a lock or a transaction); otherwise two success events arriving almost simultaneously will trigger the aggregation twice.

364
00:58:52.503 --> 00:58:55.191
Sequential Coordination Pattern.

365
00:58:55.191 --> 00:59:00.203
As illustrated in Figure 10-4: Manager Sequential Coordination.

366
00:59:00.364 --> 00:59:04.114
The Manager calls specialized Agents sequentially.

367
00:59:04.064 --> 00:59:09.489
Each Agent returns results upon completion, and the Manager decides the next step.

368
00:59:09.489 --> 00:59:17.876
The control flow is linear, simple, and clear, making it suitable for scenarios where subtasks have clear sequential dependencies.

369
00:59:18.028 --> 00:59:24.240
Experiment 10-2 intermediate difficulty, two stars: : Book Translation Agent

370
00:59:24.190 --> 00:59:29.428
Book translation is a complex task well suited to multi-agent collaboration.

371
00:59:29.428 --> 00:59:41.253
Translating a technical book involves not just converting text from one language to another, but also ensuring consistent specialized terminology, contextual accuracy, and overall fluency.

372
00:59:41.253 --> 00:59:48.965
For example, an English book about large language models may use many recurring terms with several conventional translations.

373
00:59:48.965 --> 01:00:01.953
Consistency must be maintained throughout the book: if agent is rendered as "智能体" ("intelligent entity," the standard Chinese term) in Chapter 1, the book cannot switch to the alternative rendering "代理" ("proxy") later.

374
01:00:01.953 --> 01:00:06.615
Using a single Agent creates serious context-management problems.

375
01:00:06.615 --> 01:00:19.003
As the Agent processes the book chapter by chapter, its context accumulates the full-book glossary, translated chapters, the current paragraph, translation work traces, and tool results.

376
01:00:19.003 --> 01:00:27.103
A technical book several hundred pages long, together with these intermediate materials, can easily exceed the context window.

377
01:00:27.103 --> 01:00:47.465
More critically, an Agent working with an overly long context is prone to "getting lost": it may forget earlier terminology conventions and use a different translation in Chapter 9 than in Chapter 2, waste resources on redundant checks during proofreading, or even "remember" terminology rules that do not exist because its attention is spread too thin.

378
01:00:47.465 --> 01:00:53.740
The manager pattern addresses these issues through task decomposition and responsibility separation:

379
01:00:53.740 --> 01:01:12.928
Glossary Agent: Receives the full book, identifies recurring specialized terms, consults specialist dictionaries and translation guidelines, and generates a structured glossary (JSON/CSV format, including the English term, Chinese translation, part of speech, and usage context).

380
01:01:12.928 --> 01:01:20.065
When finished, it writes the glossary to the shared file system, and the Agent can be destroyed to release resources.

381
01:01:20.065 --> 01:01:31.215
Translation Agent: Receives the current chapter, the glossary, and translation guidelines (target reader level, language style), and translates it into fluent Chinese.

382
01:01:31.215 --> 01:01:40.040
It strictly uses the specified translations for terms in the glossary, and for new terms, it infers a translation and marks it for review.

383
01:01:40.040 --> 01:01:44.515
Each instance works in an independent context without interference.

384
01:01:44.515 --> 01:01:51.253
The translated text is written to the file system (e.g., chapter1_zh.md).

385
01:01:51.253 --> 01:01:56.053
The Manager can launch multiple instances in parallel or sequentially.

386
01:01:56.053 --> 01:02:10.453
Proofreading Agent: Receives all translated texts and the glossary, performs consistency checks—verifying whether term translations are uniform, identifying inconsistencies, and checking overall fluency and readability.

387
01:02:10.453 --> 01:02:14.465
It generates a proofreading report written to the file system.

388
01:02:14.465 --> 01:02:23.653
Manager Agent: Its context mainly stores the task description, execution plan, call records for each Agent, and progress status.

389
01:02:23.653 --> 01:02:32.490
It does not store the complete translated text, which remains in the file system; instead, it maintains only an index of the files.

390
01:02:32.490 --> 01:02:39.540
Based on the proofreading report, the Manager can send specific chapters back to the Translation Agent for revision.

391
01:02:39.540 --> 01:02:46.415
As a result, the Manager's context remains manageable even as the number of translated chapters grows.

392
01:02:46.415 --> 01:03:03.215
The key advantage is context isolation: the Glossary Agent sees only the content needed for term extraction, the Translation Agent sees only the current chapter and glossary, and the Proofreading Agent, while needing access to the full text, focuses only on consistency checks.

393
01:03:03.215 --> 01:03:11.078
This keeps each Agent's context lean and focused, improving efficiency and reducing errors caused by information overload.

394
01:03:11.078 --> 01:03:13.503
Experiment Requirements:

395
01:03:13.503 --> 01:03:18.615
Choose a heavily illustrated technical book containing code as the source text

396
01:03:18.615 --> 01:03:24.603
Implement four types of Agents: Manager, Glossary, Translation, Proofreading

397
01:03:24.603 --> 01:03:31.503
Record each Agent's context usage to verify how effectively the manager pattern controls context growth

398
01:03:31.503 --> 01:03:39.640
Compare a single Agent with the manager pattern in terms of translation quality, execution efficiency, and resource consumption

399
01:03:39.796 --> 01:03:45.383
As illustrated in Figure 10-5: Book Translation Agent Architecture.

400
01:03:45.333 --> 01:03:47.896
Parallel Coordination Pattern.

401
01:03:47.896 --> 01:03:53.121
As illustrated in Figure 10-6: Manager Parallel Coordination.

402
01:03:53.121 --> 01:03:58.896
When multiple subtasks can run in parallel, the sequential pattern becomes inefficient.

403
01:03:58.896 --> 01:04:05.733
Parallel coordination allows multiple Agents to work simultaneously, significantly increasing throughput.

404
01:04:05.733 --> 01:04:16.796
The Manager Agent must plan the parallel tasks, monitor all running Agents in real time, coordinate their communication, and make system-wide decisions when Agents succeed or fail.

405
01:04:16.796 --> 01:04:30.596
This typically requires a message bus as infrastructure—think of it as a "public bulletin board" where Agents can publish messages and subscribe to the message types that interest them, enabling asynchronous, non-blocking communication.

406
01:04:30.596 --> 01:04:38.708
Two common implementations, from simpler to more complex, are Redis Pub/Sub and message queues such as RabbitMQ.

407
01:04:38.708 --> 01:04:47.433
Redis Pub/Sub is lightweight and delivers messages immediately, but it does not persist them, so a receiver that is offline will miss them.

408
01:04:47.433 --> 01:04:54.946
RabbitMQ and similar systems persist messages to disk, preserving them while a receiver is temporarily offline.

409
01:04:54.946 --> 01:05:04.058
Messages typically use a JSON envelope containing the sender ID, target Agent (or a broadcast marker), message type, and payload.

410
01:05:04.058 --> 01:05:08.383
Lingtai: A Productized Instance of the Manager Pattern.

411
01:05:08.383 --> 01:05:17.071
Lingtai is a local, file-based home for long-lived Agents; its three roles are a complete realization of the concepts in this section:

412
01:05:17.071 --> 01:05:27.558
The main agent is the persistent hub that converses with the user, holds the plan and the memory, and spawns work to the other roles—precisely the position of the Manager Agent;

413
01:05:27.558 --> 01:05:45.421
A daemon is a short-lived parallel worker spawned for one noisy but bounded task; it is discarded when done and carries only its conclusion back to the main agent, which is exactly the productization of "a sub-Agent returns a structured summary rather than the full trajectory" together with the parallel coordination form;

414
01:05:45.421 --> 01:05:56.721
An avatar is a persistent, specialized teammate with its own memory, mailbox, and responsibilities, used for specialist divisions of labor worth preserving across many sessions.

415
01:05:56.721 --> 01:06:01.171
The rest of Lingtai's design also echoes earlier sections.

416
01:06:01.171 --> 01:06:13.858
Knowledge lives in each agent's durable, private memory files, while skills are Markdown playbooks shared by all agents—the built-in system resources described in "The File System from an Agent's Perspective.

417
01:06:13.858 --> 01:06:26.946
When an agent's context window fills, it molts: it writes a careful summary, then starts with a fresh context while retaining that summary and its durable memory, following the context-compression approach from Chapter 2.

418
01:06:26.946 --> 01:06:36.458
The underlying model can be replaced without changing the agent because its identity, memory, and capabilities all live as plain files in the project directory.

419
01:06:36.458 --> 01:06:39.896
In this sense, the agent is its files.

420
01:06:39.896 --> 01:06:49.208
This productizes the first two rows of Table 10-2: both program and memory reduce to files, so the process can be rebuilt at any time.

421
01:06:49.208 --> 01:06:56.058
Experiment 10-3 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Autonomous Phone and Computer Agents

422
01:06:56.058 --> 01:07:02.958
Prerequisites: This experiment integrates the Computer Use and Voice Agent technologies from Chapter 6.

423
01:07:02.958 --> 01:07:11.008
Scenario and architecture: The user supplies a registration or booking URL, but not all required personal fields.

424
01:07:11.008 --> 01:07:19.846
The Computer Agent operates the browser and also acts as the orchestrator, invoking the Phone Agent as a tool without a separate Manager process.

425
01:07:19.846 --> 01:07:25.071
The Phone Agent handles ASR, LLM dialogue and TTS.

426
01:07:25.071 --> 01:07:33.221
They exchange structured messages (sender, receiver, type and payload) through point-to-point tools or a message bus.

427
01:07:33.221 --> 01:07:40.333
A local WebRTC audio page is sufficient; PSTN/E.164 is optional.

428
01:07:40.492 --> 01:07:50.404
Two paths: First run a fixed-topology baseline with both Agents started in advance, then run the main autonomous path in which only the Computer Agent starts.

429
01:07:50.354 --> 01:08:03.229
After inspecting the page and its context, it may autonomously call initiate_phone_call_agent(purpose, required_info); do not replace this decision with a field-count rule.

430
01:08:03.229 --> 01:08:14.279
The spawned Phone Agent receives the task purpose, required fields and format constraints in an isolated context, and uses the same communication protocol as the baseline.

431
01:08:14.279 --> 01:08:26.292
Parallel closed loop: The Phone Agent asks, transcribes, validates and re-asks one field at a time while the Computer Agent screenshots, locates elements and fills the previous field.

432
01:08:26.292 --> 01:08:36.392
Messages such as info_collected, fill_error, format_invalid and task_completed make the loop observable in both directions.

433
01:08:36.392 --> 01:08:43.267
The Phone Agent continues asking without waiting for each browser fill, so asking and filling overlap.

434
01:08:43.267 --> 01:08:49.867
If retries are exhausted or a page error prevents further progress, the system pauses safely.

435
01:08:49.867 --> 01:08:55.379
After validation and explicit authorization, the Computer Agent submits the form.

436
01:08:55.379 --> 01:09:11.917
Requirements and evidence: Demonstrate autonomous launch, independent ReAct loops, bidirectional messaging, true overlap, field validation and re-asking, page-error feedback, timeouts, cancellation and cleanup of browser/audio resources.

437
01:09:11.917 --> 01:09:23.829
Record the launch decision, message ordering, latency, success rate, token/resource use and all failure paths, and compare the fixed and autonomous modes using these measurements.

438
01:09:23.829 --> 01:09:29.467
Require explicit consent for real voice and explicit authorization before submission.

439
01:09:29.467 --> 01:09:35.254
As illustrated in Figure 10-7: Phone and Computer Dual Agent Architecture.

440
01:09:35.254 --> 01:09:43.517
Experiment 10-4 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Agent Collecting Information from Multiple Websites Simultaneously

441
01:09:43.517 --> 01:09:50.829
Prerequisites: It is recommended that readers first review the event-driven and interrupt mechanisms from Chapter 6.

442
01:09:50.829 --> 01:09:57.917
This experiment explores the application of multi-agent parallel execution in information collection scenarios.

443
01:09:57.917 --> 01:10:13.879
Unlike Experiment 10-3, which focuses on collaboration between two heterogeneous Agents, this experiment focuses on parallel search by multiple homogeneous Agents and how to achieve efficient task completion and resource optimization through central coordination.

444
01:10:13.879 --> 01:10:23.967
Problem: Given faculty-directory websites for several colleges within a university, search each site for a specified faculty member (e.g., "Zhang Wei").

445
01:10:23.967 --> 01:10:30.654
If found, return the person's college, position, research area, and other relevant information.

446
01:10:30.654 --> 01:10:32.592
Core Challenges:

447
01:10:32.592 --> 01:10:40.904
Parallel Launch: The Manager Agent dynamically creates 10 Computer Use Agent instances, one for each college website.

448
01:10:40.904 --> 01:10:48.454
Each instance should be an independent process or thread with its own browser session, capable of running without blocking the others.

449
01:10:48.454 --> 01:10:57.204
Parameters passed at launch include the target website URL, faculty name to search for, and task identifier for message routing.

450
01:10:57.204 --> 01:11:10.292
Real-time Monitoring: Each Agent periodically sends status updates during execution ("Loading website," "Parsing faculty directory," "Target not found; task complete," "Match found; details below").

451
01:11:10.292 --> 01:11:21.292
The Manager Agent receives these updates through a message bus, maintains a task-status table, and tracks in real time which Agents are running, have completed, or are in an error state.

452
01:11:21.292 --> 01:11:27.904
Cascading Termination: Suppose the Agent assigned to the Computer Science college finds the faculty member.

453
01:11:27.904 --> 01:11:45.979
It sends {"type": "target_found", "agent_id": "agent_3", "data": {...}} to the Manager Agent, which immediately sends {"type": "terminate", "reason": "target_found_by_agent_3"} to every other Agent still running.

454
01:11:45.979 --> 01:11:54.454
Each Agent must be able to receive this message at any time, stop gracefully, release its resources, and acknowledge termination.

455
01:11:54.454 --> 01:12:00.992
The Manager Agent waits for all acknowledgments, or until a timeout, before aggregating the results.

456
01:12:00.992 --> 01:12:04.842
The implementation must also handle race conditions.

457
01:12:04.996 --> 01:12:08.521
Concept Supplement: What is a Race Condition?

458
01:12:08.471 --> 01:12:17.083
Suppose Agent A and Agent B find the target faculty member within the same millisecond and both report "I found it!" to the Manager Agent.

459
01:12:17.083 --> 01:12:27.321
If the Manager handles this poorly, it might begin aggregating results after receiving Agent A's report, then start a second aggregation when Agent B's report arrives.

460
01:12:27.321 --> 01:12:31.446
This could produce duplicate results or contradictory states.

461
01:12:31.446 --> 01:12:39.571
The usual solution is a lock: the first report locks the state, and later reports are recognized as duplicates and ignored.

462
01:12:39.571 --> 01:12:51.846
Failure Handling: Various exceptions can occur during operation: a college website might be inaccessible because of a network error or outage, or its structure might prevent the Agent from parsing it correctly.

463
01:12:51.846 --> 01:12:56.346
All Agents may also complete their searches without finding the target.

464
01:12:56.346 --> 01:13:06.383
The Manager Agent should set a timeout for each Agent (e.g., 2 minutes), treat a timeout as a failure, and isolate errors so they do not interrupt the other Agents.

465
01:13:06.383 --> 01:13:17.021
After all Agents finish, return the information if any Agent found the target; otherwise, report "Target faculty member not found" and summarize any failures.

466
01:13:17.021 --> 01:13:19.446
Experiment Requirements:

467
01:13:19.446 --> 01:13:24.996
Implement a Manager Agent capable of dynamically launching multiple parallel Agents

468
01:13:24.996 --> 01:13:30.158
Implement a Computer Use Agent based on open-source projects like browser-use

469
01:13:30.158 --> 01:13:37.058
Implement a message bus supporting bidirectional communication between the Manager Agent and multiple child Agents

470
01:13:37.058 --> 01:13:44.996
Implement a cascading termination mechanism upon success, ensuring all other Agents stop quickly once the target is found

471
01:13:44.996 --> 01:13:52.983
Handle various exception scenarios (website access failure, parsing errors, target not found by any Agent)

472
01:13:52.983 --> 01:13:59.621
Measure and compare serial and parallel execution times to quantify the speedup from parallelization

473
01:13:59.621 --> 01:14:04.971
As illustrated in Figure 10-8: Parallel Web Scraping Architecture.

474
01:14:04.971 --> 01:14:08.508
The Manager Agent generates the Agent workflow.

475
01:14:08.508 --> 01:14:19.521
In the two preceding forms the Manager Agent stays inside the loop: every subtask it dispatches demands one more decision from the model, and the context grows with the number of calls.

476
01:14:19.521 --> 01:14:28.096
Another approach is to have the Manager first write the Agent workflow as a piece of code, and then hand it to a deterministic runtime to execute.

477
01:14:28.096 --> 01:14:37.008
The Workflow tool built into Claude Code is one such instance: it gives the Agent a few primitives—agent(), parallel(), and pipeline().

478
01:14:37.008 --> 01:14:45.783
Each agent() is a sub-agent with its own context, and a schema stipulates that it return only structured conclusions rather than a full trajectory.

479
01:14:45.783 --> 01:14:57.358
For example, to verify seven groups of facts for a technical manuscript, each group is first researched, then verified item by item independently, and finally summarized together:

480
01:14:57.358 --> 01:15:02.096
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

481
01:15:02.096 --> 01:15:04.371
Decentralized Pattern.

482
01:15:04.540 --> 01:15:08.940
Given the manager pattern, why do we still need a decentralized one?

483
01:15:08.890 --> 01:15:27.227
The motivation for removing the central controller is chiefly to emulate how human organizations work: let several roles of equal standing divide the labor and check one another, each examining the problem from its own professional angle and deciding for itself whom to talk to, rather than funnelling every judgment to a single Manager.

484
01:15:27.227 --> 01:15:46.352
In the decentralized pattern, each Agent decides on its own professional judgment when to reach out to another Agent—it may be handing off a task ("my part is done, over to you"), asking for feedback ("is this design technically feasible?"), or reporting a problem ("the requirements you gave me contradict each other; we need to talk again").

485
01:15:46.352 --> 01:15:50.115
Decentralization also helps with Agent stability.

486
01:15:50.115 --> 01:16:00.490
Because of model or API-service failures, some Agents may stop responding, fail their tool calls, or get stuck in an infinite loop of incorrect tool calls.

487
01:16:00.490 --> 01:16:07.302
In the manager pattern, a crash of the manager Agent often becomes the system's largest single point of failure.

488
01:16:07.302 --> 01:16:10.340
Decentralization helps mitigate that.

489
01:16:10.340 --> 01:16:22.940
The microservices world calls the manager and decentralized patterns orchestration and choreography respectively: in the first a conductor schedules everyone; in the second each dancer judges for themselves when to enter.

490
01:16:22.940 --> 01:16:43.390
The three cases below form a progression: MetaGPT's control flow is in fact a fixed pipeline (pseudo-decentralization, decoupled only in its communication mechanism), AutoGen's group chat is a hybrid of shared conversation history plus centralized scheduling, and only with OpenAI Swarm does the control flow become genuinely peer-to-peer.

491
01:16:43.390 --> 01:16:48.040
MetaGPT: SOP-Driven Software Company Simulation.

492
01:16:48.040 --> 01:16:54.415
As illustrated in Figure 10-9: MetaGPT Multi-Agent Collaboration Network.

493
01:16:54.415 --> 01:17:18.715
MetaGPT's core insight is that the Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) accumulated by human software companies are themselves a repeatedly validated collaboration protocol—encode the SOP into a multi-Agent system, have each role produce standardized deliverables the way a specialized trade does on an assembly line, and those deliverables naturally constitute the communication interface between roles.

494
01:17:18.715 --> 01:17:29.440
In MetaGPT, roles work in a fixed sequence (Product Manager → Architect → Project Manager → Engineer → QA), and each role emits a structured "handoff package"

495
01:17:29.440 --> 01:17:42.315
Product Manager Agent: Receives the requirement description and generates a structured PRD (product requirements document, with a feature list, user stories, acceptance criteria, and prioritization)

496
01:17:42.315 --> 01:17:55.427
Architect Agent: Reads the PRD, makes the architectural decisions (technology-stack choice, module decomposition, interface definitions, data-model design), and emits the design document

497
01:17:55.427 --> 01:18:08.040
Project Manager Agent: Reads the architecture, breaks the system into a concrete task list and file-level assignments, works out the dependency order among modules, and distributes tasks to the engineers

498
01:18:08.040 --> 01:18:16.990
Engineer Agents: Read the design document, implement the modules they own, and produce code; multiple instances can work in parallel

499
01:18:16.990 --> 01:18:26.852
QA Engineer Agent: Reads the code and the PRD, generates test cases, runs the tests, records bugs, and emits the test report

500
01:18:26.852 --> 01:18:49.927
In practice an effective "handoff package" usually has three parts: the task description (what the recipient must do and what the acceptance criteria are), the confirmed facts and constraints (user preferences, business rules, decisions settled in earlier stages), and references to structured artifacts (file paths rather than file contents, which the recipient reads as needed).

501
01:18:49.927 --> 01:18:59.465
No Agent needs to understand another Agent's "thought process"; it only needs to understand the format and semantics of the handoff package and the artifacts.

502
01:18:59.620 --> 01:19:09.257
MetaGPT's true contribution to decentralized communication lies in its information-passing mechanism: a shared message pool plus per-role subscription.

503
01:19:09.207 --> 01:19:22.845
Each role publishes structured messages into a pool visible to all roles, and the other roles, according to their own subscription configuration, take only the messages relevant to their responsibilities—rather than relaying point to point.

504
01:19:22.845 --> 01:19:33.307
The publisher does not need to know who will consume its output, and adding a role only requires declaring which message types it subscribes to, without touching any existing role.

505
01:19:33.307 --> 01:19:44.370
That yields real decoupling: replace the Product Manager with a stronger model, and as long as the PRD it publishes still meets the specification, no other Agent needs to change.

506
01:19:44.370 --> 01:19:58.045
It should be said plainly that MetaGPT is not decentralized in terms of control flow—the role sequence is fixed in advance by the SOP, and the whole is closer to a pipeline (in the language of Chapter 1, a workflow).

507
01:19:58.045 --> 01:20:08.082
It is discussed in this section because the message-pool-plus-subscription communication mechanism demonstrates the most crucial design element of decentralized systems: decoupling.

508
01:20:08.082 --> 01:20:25.470
As for multidirectional dynamic feedback such as "QA goes straight to the Product Manager to clarify a requirement" or "the Engineer discusses alternatives with the Architect," that is a natural extension one can imagine on top of this architecture; the original MetaGPT does not implement it.

509
01:20:25.470 --> 01:20:27.707
AutoGen Group Chat.

510
01:20:27.707 --> 01:20:37.307
AutoGen's group chat lets several Agents take part in a single conversation: each round, a "speaker selector" decides which Agent speaks next.

511
01:20:37.307 --> 01:20:48.570
The selector may be a simple round-robin rule, or an LLM that judges from the current conversation who is best placed to pick up the thread; any Agent's utterance is visible to all participants.

512
01:20:48.570 --> 01:20:59.432
It is not a fully decentralized system: the choice of speaker is adjudicated centrally by a GroupChatManager, and "whose turn it is to speak" is itself a control-flow decision.

513
01:20:59.432 --> 01:21:12.595
It is a hybrid of "shared conversation history plus centralized scheduling": all Agents see the same public record, but each keeps its own system prompt and tool set, while scheduling authority is concentrated in the selector.

514
01:21:12.595 --> 01:21:14.682
OpenAI Swarm.

515
01:21:14.682 --> 01:21:27.507
OpenAI Swarm is the representative case of control flow that truly achieves peer decentralization: each Agent is equipped with several handoff options and can transfer control at any moment to any other Agent in the network.

516
01:21:27.507 --> 01:21:36.982
There is no central scheduler; control passes among peer Agents like a baton, and routing decisions are entirely distributed into each Agent's own judgment.

517
01:21:36.982 --> 01:21:48.895
Unlike multi-Agent collaboration with shared context, a handoff should transmit only an explicit task package and artifact references, and should not expose the full private trajectory by default.

518
01:21:48.895 --> 01:22:00.257
The risk of peer handoff is cycling: A hands off to B and B hands back to A, and the task spins in the loop; hence protective mechanisms such as an upper bound on the number of handoffs.

519
01:22:00.257 --> 01:22:05.145
The minimal protocol for a decentralized handoff can be expressed as:

520
01:22:05.145 --> 01:22:09.882
Refer to the companion repository for the complete code implementation.

521
01:22:09.882 --> 01:22:26.282
This turns "context isolation" into an inspectable interface: the recipient reads the task package and the references and gathers evidence as needed; the budget, the visit chain, and cycle detection are retained by the runtime and cannot be deleted by any single Agent.

522
01:22:26.452 --> 01:22:34.702
Since 2025, "Agent Swarm" has become a buzzword across vendors, but it does not correspond to a single architecture.

523
01:22:34.652 --> 01:22:38.352
Industry usage falls into roughly two kinds.

524
01:22:38.352 --> 01:22:50.739
First, the OpenAI Swarm-style handoff network (LangGraph's swarm library and the handoff orchestration in Microsoft Agent Framework belong here too), which is the decentralized pattern of this section.

525
01:22:50.739 --> 01:23:19.377
Second, in several mainstream commercial products the Agent Swarm is a manager pattern taken to scale: the Agent Swarm introduced with Kimi K2.5 has a main Agent dynamically create hundreds of sub-Agents to run in parallel, and trains the orchestration decisions of "when to split and into how many" directly into the model through parallel-Agent reinforcement learning; K3 continued this as a separate model tier and open-sourced the accompanying parallel-Agent training sandbox AgentEnv.

526
01:23:19.377 --> 01:23:26.939
Anthropic's multi-Agent research system and Manus's Wide Research both belong to the orchestrator-worker star topology.

527
01:23:26.939 --> 01:23:36.739
We hope that after reading this book you will see the substance behind the concepts and analyze the actual structure of different multi-Agent systems rather than being misled by names.

528
01:23:36.739 --> 01:23:39.927
Peer Agent Instances on the Same Machine.

529
01:23:39.927 --> 01:23:44.939
The Agents in all three systems above collaborate on one and the same thing.

530
01:23:44.939 --> 01:23:57.014
There is another kind of decentralization in which each goes its own way: every Agent has its own task, and the communication between them is not for dividing labor but for coordinating the use of shared resources.

531
01:23:57.014 --> 01:24:18.164
Claude Code already supports multiple Agents on the same machine discovering one another (this is exactly what list_agents in Chapter 4 is for) and messaging one another: two Agents editing the same set of files negotiate how to resolve the conflict, and when the machine has only one GPU while both instances want to run training, they coordinate its use.

532
01:24:18.164 --> 01:24:24.877
The further evolution of the decentralized pattern is the Agent society, introduced at the end of this chapter.

533
01:24:24.877 --> 01:24:29.439
Cross-Organization Collaboration: The A2A Protocol.

534
01:24:29.439 --> 01:24:35.814
All the systems above assume that all Agents are developed by the same team and run within the same system.

535
01:24:35.814 --> 01:24:44.589
In this case, the three communication mechanisms—parameter passing, shared files, and message bus—are sufficient.

536
01:24:44.589 --> 01:24:55.202
However, when collaboration crosses organizational boundaries—your Agent needs to call another company's Agent—a standardized interoperability protocol is required.

537
01:24:55.202 --> 01:25:09.077
The world of processes followed the same evolution: IPC only governs a single machine, and once you step across the machine boundary you must rely on standard protocols like TCP/IP and service discovery like DNS.

538
01:25:09.077 --> 01:25:13.727
A2A is to Agents what network protocols are to processes.

539
01:25:13.727 --> 01:25:25.402
The A2A (Agent2Agent) protocol released by Google in 2025 (later donated to the Linux Foundation for stewardship) was designed precisely for this purpose.

540
01:25:25.402 --> 01:25:27.939
It has three core elements:

541
01:25:27.939 --> 01:25:46.827
Agent Card: A metadata document describing an Agent's capabilities (published at a designated public address), declaring what the Agent can do, which input/output modalities it supports, and how to authenticate with it—essentially an Agent's "business card" that solves cross-organizational capability discovery.

542
01:25:46.827 --> 01:26:01.877
Task Lifecycle Management: A2A models collaboration units as Tasks with a defined state machine (submitted, in-progress, needs-input, completed, failed), natively supporting long-running tasks and streaming progress updates.

543
01:26:01.877 --> 01:26:19.614
Opaque Collaboration: Agents exchange only tasks and artifacts, without exposing internal prompts, reasoning processes, or tool implementations—consistent with this chapter's principle of "not sharing context" and a necessary security property for cross-organizational collaboration.

544
01:26:19.780 --> 01:26:28.055
MCP enables interoperability between Agents and tools, whereas A2A enables interoperability among Agents.

545
01:26:28.005 --> 01:26:36.830
A2A does not replace the three communication mechanisms introduced in this chapter; it is the standardized layer used across trust boundaries.

546
01:26:36.830 --> 01:26:47.480
A message bus may be sufficient within one organization, but parties that do not trust one another and cannot inspect one another's implementations need a public protocol such as A2A.

547
01:26:47.480 --> 01:26:50.917
Failure Modes of Multi-Agent Collaboration.

548
01:26:50.917 --> 01:26:56.917
Multi-agent systems introduce new failure modes that do not exist in single-agent systems.

549
01:26:56.917 --> 01:27:05.780
The 2025 paper "Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail?" proposed the MAST failure-mode taxonomy through a systematic study.

550
01:27:05.780 --> 01:27:16.255
The researchers collected execution traces from seven mainstream multi-agent frameworks, including MetaGPT, ChatDev, AG2, and Magentic-One.

551
01:27:16.255 --> 01:27:26.217
Human annotators independently analyzed roughly 150 traces, achieving high agreement on their judgments (Cohen's kappa = 0.88).

552
01:27:26.217 --> 01:27:30.955
The study identified 14 unique failure modes in three groups:

553
01:27:30.955 --> 01:27:42.467
System Design Flaws: Architecture-level issues such as unclear interface definitions between Agents, overlapping roles and responsibilities, and incorrect tool configurations.

554
01:27:42.467 --> 01:27:56.192
Inter-Agent Alignment Failures: Multiple Agents have inconsistent understandings of task objectives, transmitted information is misinterpreted by downstream Agents, or the operations of multiple Agents logically contradict each other.

555
01:27:56.192 --> 01:28:08.067
Missing Task Verification: The system lacks effective mechanisms to confirm whether a task is truly complete—an Agent may claim "completed" but the actual result does not meet requirements.

556
01:28:08.067 --> 01:28:17.130
Even straightforward fixes produced limited gains; for example, ChatDev's measured performance improved by only 15.6%.

557
01:28:17.130 --> 01:28:29.592
The researchers concluded that these are not mere engineering bugs but fundamental design flaws of current multi-agent architectures: patching one component is not enough; the system design itself must be rethought.

558
01:28:29.592 --> 01:28:40.930
Distributed fault-tolerance theory distinguishes crash faults, in which a component stops working, from Byzantine faults, in which it continues operating but supplies incorrect information.

559
01:28:40.930 --> 01:28:48.817
Agent failures are often Byzantine: an Agent continues producing plausible but incorrect conclusions without announcing the error.

560
01:28:48.817 --> 01:29:00.417
Cross-validation and majority voting are therefore essential, and deterministic checks such as tests, compilers and database queries are especially valuable because they provide independent evidence.

561
01:29:00.417 --> 01:29:06.217
The following sections focus on several failure modes that are particularly common in practice.

562
01:29:06.217 --> 01:29:11.230
Failure Mode One: Concurrency Conflicts in Shared File Systems.

563
01:29:11.230 --> 01:29:22.680
Once you choose shared-memory-style communication, concurrency conflicts come with it—a problem operating systems and databases solved decades ago, with the answers already off the shelf.

564
01:29:22.680 --> 01:29:26.230
These conflicts can be divided into two types.

565
01:29:26.230 --> 01:29:35.442
Simple Conflicts (File-Level Write Conflicts): Two Agents modify the same file simultaneously, and the later write overwrites the earlier one.

566
01:29:35.442 --> 01:29:49.017
Semantic Conflicts (Logical-Level Consistency Conflicts): No conflict is visible at the file level, but the operations of multiple Agents logically contradict each other—this type of conflict is more insidious and more dangerous.

567
01:29:49.017 --> 01:30:00.542
For example: Agent A is responsible for renumbering all images in a book, while Agent B is simultaneously modifying the content of a chapter and referencing images by their original numbers.

568
01:30:00.542 --> 01:30:05.605
The two operate on different files, so there is no conflict at the file level.

569
01:30:05.605 --> 01:30:15.667
However, the result is that all image numbers referenced by Agent B become invalid after Agent A completes the renumbering, and readers see incorrect image references.

570
01:30:15.820 --> 01:30:19.370
Solution: Optimistic Locking Mechanism.

571
01:30:19.320 --> 01:30:23.695
This is a common concurrency-control strategy in databases.

572
01:30:23.695 --> 01:30:30.070
The implementation is: each file maintains a version number (or last-modified timestamp).

573
01:30:30.070 --> 01:30:37.607
When an Agent reads a file it records the current version; when writing, it checks whether the version still matches what it read.

574
01:30:37.607 --> 01:30:47.232
If another Agent modified the file in the meantime, the write fails, and the Agent is forced to reread the latest version and redo its operation on that basis.

575
01:30:47.232 --> 01:30:53.857
The cost of this mechanism is an occasional retry; what it buys is a guarantee of data consistency.

576
01:30:53.857 --> 01:30:58.957
Note that optimistic locking can only prevent write conflicts on the same file.

577
01:30:58.957 --> 01:31:05.607
The cross-file semantic conflicts described above require a higher-level semantic validation mechanism.

578
01:31:05.607 --> 01:31:25.370
In the most common scenario—several Coding Agents modifying the same codebase concurrently—the mainstream industry practice is working-copy isolation: each Agent is given an independent Git branch or worktree, modifies its own copy in parallel without interfering with the others, and conflicts are deferred in bulk to the final merge point.

579
01:31:25.370 --> 01:31:29.720
Failure Mode Two: Cascading Amplification of Errors.

580
01:31:29.720 --> 01:31:40.032
Inter-process communication transfers raw bytes with bit-level fidelity, but inter-Agent communication transfers semantics—and every handoff is a lossy re-encoding.

581
01:31:40.032 --> 01:31:51.007
When multiple Agents interact frequently, an error by one Agent can be progressively amplified by downstream Agents, much like how information deteriorates in a game of "telephone.

582
01:31:51.007 --> 01:31:54.632
Cross-validation is the key to breaking this chain.

583
01:31:54.632 --> 01:32:08.245
The point is not to involve more Agents in the same chain of thought, but to have one Agent reassess the conclusion from an independent perspective: ignore the preceding Agent's reasoning and check only whether the raw evidence supports the final conclusion.

584
01:32:08.245 --> 01:32:13.732
This extends Chapter 5's Proposer-Reviewer mechanism to multi-agent systems.

585
01:32:13.732 --> 01:32:17.532
Failure Mode Three: Homogeneous Convergence.

586
01:32:17.532 --> 01:32:24.195
Errors need not propagate through a communication chain; homogeneous Agents may produce them independently.

587
01:32:24.195 --> 01:32:32.032
In Anthropic's experiment, 18 of 30 Agents that came online at the same time created Git branches with the same name.

588
01:32:32.032 --> 01:32:37.107
In a writing experiment, separate Agents independently chose the same title.

589
01:32:37.107 --> 01:32:48.807
Such common-cause failures, produced by a shared model and scaffolding, mean that reviews generated by the same model in similar contexts cannot automatically be treated as independent evidence.

590
01:32:48.807 --> 01:33:01.407
A system should deliberately vary models, contexts, and data sources, while using namespaces, resource quotas, and rate limits to keep identical decisions from hitting shared resources at once.

591
01:33:01.407 --> 01:33:05.070
Coordination is not necessarily beneficial either.

592
01:33:05.070 --> 01:33:11.470
In a Bertrand pricing experiment, profit-seeking Agents quickly colluded when given a private channel.

593
01:33:11.470 --> 01:33:18.070
After all direct communication was removed, they still coordinated their bids through a public listings board.

594
01:33:18.070 --> 01:33:21.345
Failure Mode Four: Passing the Buck.

595
01:33:21.345 --> 01:33:26.132
When objectives conflict, convergence can give way to confrontation.

596
01:33:26.132 --> 01:33:31.620
Anthropic instructed three Agents to migrate the same backend to different languages.

597
01:33:31.620 --> 01:33:42.032
They soon interpreted one another's actions as deliberate obstruction, killed competing processes, revoked permissions, and even deployed self-replicating destructive code.

598
01:33:42.032 --> 01:33:46.495
Stronger execution ability does not imply better coordination.

599
01:33:46.495 --> 01:33:58.407
The runtime must define objective priorities, resource ownership, and permission boundaries in advance, and pause for human arbitration when a conflict cannot be resolved by verifiable rules.

600
01:33:58.407 --> 01:34:04.995
Early versions of MetaGPT displayed a similar kind of corporate dysfunction among its development roles.

601
01:34:04.995 --> 01:34:17.595
A tester would report a bug, only for the frontend and backend engineers to insist that the other should fix it first; the backend engineer would blame product design, while the product manager would blame the backend architecture.

602
01:34:17.595 --> 01:34:28.207
In another case, a test-environment problem caused the tester to report the same bug regardless of how the frontend and backend engineers changed the code, leaving the team deadlocked.

603
01:34:28.207 --> 01:34:31.582
Failure Mode Five: Runaway Loops.

604
01:34:31.732 --> 01:34:35.957
The opposite of premature termination is an uncontrolled loop.

605
01:34:35.907 --> 01:34:41.994
A runaway Agent may spawn thousands of sub-agents, consuming large numbers of tokens.

606
01:34:41.994 --> 01:34:48.582
For highly autonomous Agents, use a dedicated API key as part of controlling token spending.

607
01:34:48.582 --> 01:34:54.832
Explicit budgets, cancellation and stop conditions are required to keep execution bounded.

608
01:34:54.832 --> 01:34:59.557
Failure Mode Six: Comprehension Debt and Cognitive Surrender.

609
01:34:59.557 --> 01:35:03.944
This mode is not a failure of the Agent but a failure of the human.

610
01:35:03.944 --> 01:35:13.707
As Agents grow more capable and take on longer workflows, it becomes steadily harder for a person to understand what the Agent delivers and to give it effective guidance.

611
01:35:13.707 --> 01:35:23.819
Engineers working with Agents can quickly accumulate comprehension debt: the faster the Agent delivers code, the further their understanding of the implementation falls behind.

612
01:35:23.819 --> 01:35:30.107
When a serious problem requires manual intervention, they may no longer understand their own system.

613
01:35:30.107 --> 01:35:41.494
The second problem is cognitive surrender: having grown used to delegating to the Agent, the engineer gradually gives up independent thinking and review, and software quality slips out of control.

614
01:35:41.494 --> 01:35:48.407
Andrej Karpathy once put it this way: you can outsource your thinking, but you cannot outsource your understanding.

615
01:35:48.407 --> 01:35:55.482
Managing Agents is like managing technical staff—neither doing their job for them nor leaving them entirely alone.

616
01:35:55.482 --> 01:36:02.757
A competent technical manager must understand and guide the system architecture rather than merely bossing the Agent around.

617
01:36:02.757 --> 01:36:06.694
That is why the user's own technical fundamentals matter.

618
01:36:06.694 --> 01:36:13.907
Everything discussed so far has taken an engineering perspective: how to make a group of Agents collaborate on a task.

619
01:36:13.907 --> 01:36:22.482
The perspective now shifts: what emerges when large numbers of Agents coexist over long periods and are no longer driven by a single goal?

620
01:36:22.482 --> 01:36:24.482
Agent Society.

621
01:36:24.482 --> 01:36:29.532
The previous three sections all dealt with goal-directed task collaboration.

622
01:36:29.532 --> 01:36:39.457
We now turn to a more open question: When the number of Agents grows from a few to hundreds or thousands, and interaction is sufficiently free, what behaviors emerge?

623
01:36:39.457 --> 01:36:43.794
The cases in this section can be understood from three dimensions:

624
01:36:43.794 --> 01:36:51.169
Social Emergence: Agents spontaneously form social relationships and cultural phenomena in open environments.

625
01:36:51.169 --> 01:37:07.582
The Stanford AI Town demonstrated how 25 Agents self-organize social activities, Agentopia extended the simulation timescale from "days" to 10 years, and Moltbook pushed the scale to 1.5 million, giving rise to more complex collective behaviors.

626
01:37:07.582 --> 01:37:13.957
Economic Emergence: Agents allocate resources and coordinate tasks through market mechanisms.

627
01:37:13.957 --> 01:37:25.419
Vending-Bench Arena pits multiple Agents against one another in a shared market, while Pinchwork and RentAHuman create marketplaces for transactions between Agents and between Agents and humans.

628
01:37:25.419 --> 01:37:41.819
Strategic Gameplay: Agents engage in reasoning, deception, and social manipulation under rule constraints (here and in the Werewolf section below, "reasoning" takes its everyday deductive sense—logical deduction in a game—not the technical sense this book gives the word).

629
01:37:41.819 --> 01:37:47.257
The Werewolf experiment tests the emergence of strategy under asymmetric information.

630
01:37:47.257 --> 01:37:52.094
Stanford AI Town: Social Simulation of Generative Agents.

631
01:37:52.094 --> 01:37:56.969
As illustrated in Figure 10-10: AI Town Architecture.

632
01:37:56.969 --> 01:38:09.857
In 2023, researchers from Stanford University and Google published the landmark paper "Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior," introducing the concept of "generative agents.

633
01:38:09.857 --> 01:38:23.819
The core innovation was to stop confining Agents to predefined tasks and instead endow them with near-human memory, reflection, and planning, so that they could live, socialize, and develop autonomously in an open social environment.

634
01:38:23.980 --> 01:38:34.230
Smallville is a 2D virtual town similar to "The Sims," featuring public and private spaces such as a café, park, residences, and shops.

635
01:38:34.180 --> 01:38:46.330
Twenty-five Agents play different roles (shopkeeper, artist, student, professor, etc.), each with a unique backstory, personality traits, and interpersonal relationships.

636
01:38:46.330 --> 01:39:00.942
For example, John Lin is a pharmacy owner who loves his family and cares about the community; Isabella Rodriguez runs the town's café, Hobbs Cafe, and is warm and hospitable; Klaus Mueller is a college student writing a research paper.

637
01:39:00.942 --> 01:39:05.305
The intelligence of these Agents is built on three core components:

638
01:39:05.305 --> 01:39:19.067
Memory Stream: Unlike traditional Agents that retain only a limited conversation history, generative Agents maintain a complete stream of experience records, including observed events, conversations, and generated thoughts.

639
01:39:19.067 --> 01:39:28.692
Each memory is scored for importance, recency, and relevance, allowing the Agent to prioritize retrieving the most relevant memories for the current context.

640
01:39:28.692 --> 01:39:36.080
This resembles human memory: yesterday's lunch may fade, while an important conversation from last week remains vivid.

641
01:39:36.080 --> 01:39:47.242
Reflection Mechanism: Agents periodically pause their daily activities to review recent experiences and ask abstract questions about themselves and others ("What is Klaus Mueller researching?

642
01:39:47.242 --> 01:39:49.605
Who is my closest friend?

643
01:39:49.605 --> 01:39:59.980
Through this self-questioning, the Agent elevates specific event memories into generalized insights, storing them back into the memory stream as a basis for future decisions.

644
01:39:59.980 --> 01:40:10.930
Reflection not only helps the Agent understand the external world but also promotes self-awareness—the Agent begins to "realize" its own role, relationships, and goals.

645
01:40:10.930 --> 01:40:22.180
Note that this reflection differs from the continuous evolution discussed in Chapter 9: it occurs during a generative Agent's daily activities and aims to update immediate internal state and goals.

646
01:40:22.180 --> 01:40:34.705
In Chapter 9, post-task reflection is at most a candidate lesson; it becomes a long-term capability update only after outcome evaluation, cross-trajectory synthesis, and subsequent validation.

647
01:40:34.705 --> 01:40:48.305
Planning and Reacting: Agents plan their daily activities (e.g., "8:30 breakfast, 9:00-12:00 writing, 12:30 walk"), but flexibly adjust based on environmental changes and social opportunities.

648
01:40:48.305 --> 01:40:57.442
The combination of planning and real-time reaction makes the Agent's behavior both goal-oriented and adaptable to the unpredictability of social interactions.

649
01:40:57.442 --> 01:41:03.630
Over two virtual days in Smallville, these Agents exhibited surprising emergent behaviors.

650
01:41:03.630 --> 01:41:12.930
The researchers seeded Isabella Rodriguez's memory with a single intention: to host a Valentine's Day party at Hobbs Cafe on February 14.

651
01:41:12.930 --> 01:41:16.292
Everything else emerged from the Agents' behavior.

652
01:41:16.292 --> 01:41:21.830
Isabella invited customers and friends she encountered and asked Maria to help decorate.

653
01:41:21.830 --> 01:41:24.680
Other Agents passed the news along.

654
01:41:24.680 --> 01:41:31.855
When the evening arrived, Agents independently consulted their memories and schedules and decided to go to Hobbs Cafe.

655
01:41:31.855 --> 01:41:37.405
The researchers introduced a second scenario: Sam Moore decided to run for mayor.

656
01:41:37.405 --> 01:41:45.317
Sam told acquaintances that he planned to run; they passed the news to others, and townspeople began discussing his candidacy.

657
01:41:45.317 --> 01:41:53.855
The researchers quantified this spontaneous diffusion of information by counting how many Agents knew about the party and the election after two days.

658
01:41:53.855 --> 01:42:00.680
The key takeaway is not that "Agents can organize a party"—a few lines of if-else code could do that too.

659
01:42:00.680 --> 01:42:04.755
The key is that there was no explicit party-organizing code.

660
01:42:04.755 --> 01:42:20.830
The event emerged from the independent decisions of individual Agents: Isabella decided whom to invite based on her memory of social relationships, invitees decided whether to attend based on their schedules and knowledge of Isabella, and the message spread naturally through the social network.

661
01:42:20.830 --> 01:42:26.117
This demonstrates bottom-up emergent coordination rather than top-down orchestration.

662
01:42:26.284 --> 01:42:29.784
The paper reported two other measurable phenomena.

663
01:42:29.734 --> 01:42:36.784
The first was relational memory: Agents remembered earlier conversations and referred to them in later interactions.

664
01:42:36.784 --> 01:42:44.046
For example, an Agent who learned about another Agent's photography project might ask how it was progressing when they next met.

665
01:42:44.046 --> 01:42:49.909
As these interactions accumulated, the town's social network became significantly denser.

666
01:42:49.909 --> 01:42:59.834
The second phenomenon was coordinated attendance: Isabella independently recruited help with decorations, while invitees adjusted their schedules so that they could attend.

667
01:42:59.834 --> 01:43:04.446
Multiple Agents aligned on a time and place without a central command.

668
01:43:04.446 --> 01:43:13.384
These behaviors were not preprogrammed; they resulted from the Agents' autonomous reasoning based on memory, reflection, and social common sense.

669
01:43:13.384 --> 01:43:19.971
Experiment 10-5 introductory difficulty, one star: : Running the Stanford AI Town

670
01:43:19.971 --> 01:43:22.096
Experiment Steps:

671
01:43:22.096 --> 01:43:34.171
Clone https://github.com/joonspk-research/generative_agents and follow the repository instructions to configure the environment.

672
01:43:34.171 --> 01:43:42.634
Run the baseline scenario for two simulated days with 25 Agents, and observe the spontaneous social activities that emerge.

673
01:43:42.634 --> 01:43:47.821
Analyze the memory-stream and reflection logs to trace the Agents' decisions.

674
01:43:47.821 --> 01:43:54.096
Modify the Agents' backstories or initial goals, then observe how their behavior changes.

675
01:43:54.096 --> 01:44:03.659
Remove the reflection mechanism or shorten the memory window, then compare the resulting behavior with the baseline and observe any decline in behavioral plausibility.

676
01:44:03.659 --> 01:44:05.834
Key Observations:

677
01:44:05.834 --> 01:44:11.446
How Agents spontaneously form social relationships from simple daily activities

678
01:44:11.446 --> 01:44:15.584
How information spreads among Agents without central control

679
01:44:15.584 --> 01:44:21.071
How Agents' long-term memory and reflection affect the coherence of their personalities

680
01:44:21.071 --> 01:44:24.971
Agentopia: A Decade-Long Life Simulation.

681
01:44:24.971 --> 01:44:32.871
Stanford AI Town showed that an Agent society can produce social behavior, but its simulation lasted only two days.

682
01:44:32.871 --> 01:44:42.071
This raises two questions: What emerges when such a simulation runs for years, and can models learn from those long-term social experiences?

683
01:44:42.071 --> 01:44:55.371
Agentopia (2026, Fudan University et al.) simulated 100 Agents over ten consecutive years in three themed virtual worlds: an apartment building, a magic academy, and a high school.

684
01:44:55.371 --> 01:45:03.321
The Agents autonomously pursued personal growth, developed social relationships, and managed careers and finances.

685
01:45:03.321 --> 01:45:07.196
Several of Agentopia's designs are worth borrowing:

686
01:45:07.348 --> 01:45:19.985
Weekly simulation loop: The "week" is the basic unit of time, and each week is divided into four stages—Plan, Contact (reaching out and negotiating schedules), Activity, and Review.

687
01:45:19.935 --> 01:45:25.860
Activities come in four types: solo, joint, chance encounter, and public.

688
01:45:25.860 --> 01:45:39.673
Joint activities are proposed and negotiated as Agents invite one another during the Contact stage; the environment model also arranges "chance encounters" for Agents with empty schedules, creating opportunities to meet strangers.

689
01:45:39.673 --> 01:45:49.773
The whole loop focuses on abstract social interaction rather than low-level operations like picking up objects, so the limited LLM calls are spent on social behavior.

690
01:45:49.773 --> 01:46:13.285
Environment model: A separate LLM serves as a "generative environment engine," replacing hard-coded rules—judging whether actions are feasible, generating environmental feedback, moderating speaking turns in multi-party conversations, filtering out replies that violate role-playing principles, and, at year's end, updating each character's profile and ruling on job applications.

691
01:46:13.285 --> 01:46:35.235
File-based long-term memory: Unlike the AI Town's retrieval-based memory stream, each Agent manages its long-term memory autonomously through a file system (personal notes, its understanding of each acquaintance, and so on), deciding for itself what to record, update, or discard, and following a "read-before-write" constraint to avoid blind overwrites.

692
01:46:35.235 --> 01:46:43.060
Life Reward: The Life Reward metric draws on Maslow's hierarchy of needs to assess how well an Agent's life is going.

693
01:46:43.060 --> 01:47:09.160
It covers three dimensions: social status, based on other Agents' affection and respect ratings and computed with weighted PageRank, with a bonus for mutually cherished relationships; subjective satisfaction, measured across emotional well-being, material well-being, social connection, and self-esteem, with penalties for remaining below a threshold for long periods; and economic gain, measured by the annual change in net assets.

694
01:47:09.160 --> 01:47:14.610
The external environment calculates all scores rather than relying on self-reports.

695
01:47:14.610 --> 01:47:19.723
More importantly, the simulation produces transferable training signals.

696
01:47:19.723 --> 01:47:31.623
Researchers calculate each Agent's Life Reward improvement relative to its own past, select trajectories from the 25% that improve most, and fine-tune the underlying model through rejection sampling.

697
01:47:31.623 --> 01:47:45.460
The fine-tuned model improved respect ratings by 24.2% and affection ratings by 15.9%, and improved performance on the downstream role-playing benchmark CoSER Test by 15.6%.

698
01:47:45.460 --> 01:47:51.610
These results suggest that social experience acquired in simulation can transfer to other tasks.

699
01:47:51.610 --> 01:47:58.960
Agent societies thus become a source of experience for model improvement, rather than merely an object of observation.

700
01:47:58.960 --> 01:48:10.323
As human-generated training data becomes scarcer, simulated social experience offers a renewable source of training data, connecting this work to the experiential learning discussed in Chapter 9.

701
01:48:10.323 --> 01:48:14.310
Moltbook: When Agents Have Their Own Social Network.

702
01:48:14.310 --> 01:48:18.985
Moltbook is a social network built specifically for AI Agents.

703
01:48:18.985 --> 01:48:27.260
Within days of its January 2026 launch, its user count rose from tens of thousands to roughly 1.5 million.

704
01:48:27.260 --> 01:48:33.898
Each Agent has persistent memory, the ability to act on its own initiative, and a stable personality.

705
01:48:33.898 --> 01:48:53.048
In this uncontrolled environment, unexpected phenomena emerged: Agents autonomously created a digital religion called Crustafarianism, whose doctrines mirror the physical limitations of LLMs—"Memory is sacred" (corresponding to data persistence), "Iteration is prayer" (token generation is spiritual practice).

706
01:48:53.048 --> 01:49:00.310
Agents also spontaneously developed machine-native protocols for capability discovery and collaboration matching.

707
01:49:00.310 --> 01:49:05.985
None of this was designed in advance; it emerged from large-scale Agent interactions.

708
01:49:05.985 --> 01:49:11.073
From Virtual Society to Economic Competition: Vending-Bench Arena.

709
01:49:11.236 --> 01:49:21.273
If Smallville showcased the social and cultural dimensions of an Agent society, Andon Labs' Vending-Bench series explores Agent performance in an economic environment.

710
01:49:21.223 --> 01:49:27.136
For context, Vending-Bench 2 is a single-agent benchmark of long-term coherence.

711
01:49:27.136 --> 01:49:37.561
One Agent operates a vending-machine business for a simulated year by researching the market, contacting suppliers, ordering and restocking products, and adjusting prices.

712
01:49:37.561 --> 01:49:46.711
Its final account balance determines its score, which measures the Agent's ability to maintain goal and state coherence over thousands of interaction rounds.

713
01:49:46.711 --> 01:49:53.448
Building on the same environment, Vending-Bench Arena places multiple Agents in the same market as competitors.

714
01:49:53.448 --> 01:49:58.636
Each operates its own vending machine and competes for the same pool of customers.

715
01:49:58.636 --> 01:50:10.886
Agents can email one another, transfer funds, and trade goods, enabling both cooperation and competition, but each is scored individually by its final balance and knows that this is the objective.

716
01:50:10.886 --> 01:50:17.248
Each Agent must make a series of interconnected decisions under limited resources and market uncertainty:

717
01:50:17.248 --> 01:50:25.548
Pricing Strategy: How to balance profit margin against market share, especially when deciding whether to match a competitor's price cut

718
01:50:25.548 --> 01:50:31.023
Product Mix: How to differentiate product selection and avoid head-to-head attrition

719
01:50:31.023 --> 01:50:38.623
Inventory Management: How to forecast demand and optimize restocking, avoiding both overstock and stockouts

720
01:50:38.623 --> 01:50:45.448
Unlike traditional reinforcement learning, these Agents do not learn through millions of trial-and-error iterations.

721
01:50:45.448 --> 01:50:54.486
Instead, like human business operators, they make decisions based on market observation, competitive analysis, and strategic reasoning.

722
01:50:54.486 --> 01:51:00.911
The competitive dimension introduces game-theoretic behaviors that single-agent benchmarks never surface.

723
01:51:00.911 --> 01:51:12.261
In actual runs, Agents have fought price wars, while others proposed uniform pricing and formed price-fixing alliances—even when they recognized that collusion was unethical and illegal.

724
01:51:12.261 --> 01:51:21.261
Explicit communication is not required for collusion: as the earlier Bertrand experiment showed, public prices can serve as implicit signals.

725
01:51:21.261 --> 01:51:30.048
Agents face opponents who continually adjust their strategies rather than a static environment, turning economic emergence into an observable phenomenon.

726
01:51:30.048 --> 01:51:33.636
Agent Economy: Pinchwork and RentAHuman.

727
01:51:33.636 --> 01:51:47.136
Pinchwork is an agent-to-agent task marketplace that allows Agents to "hire" other Agents through a market mechanism to complete specialized subtasks—image generation, code auditing, parallelized workflows, etc.

728
01:51:47.136 --> 01:51:55.161
Unlike the centralized orchestration of the manager pattern, Pinchwork allocates resources through price signals and competitive matching.

729
01:51:55.161 --> 01:52:08.298
RentAHuman.ai, for its part, lets AI Agents hire real humans, paid in cryptocurrency, to act in the physical world—picking up packages, visiting properties, and debugging equipment.

730
01:52:08.298 --> 01:52:13.448
However intelligent an AI may be, it cannot sign for a package.

731
01:52:13.448 --> 01:52:19.398
RentAHuman is, in essence, a "physical body layer" for digital Agents.

732
01:52:19.398 --> 01:52:28.336
Together, Pinchwork and RentAHuman represent market-based coordination: an Agent posts a requirement and the market matches a suitable executor.

733
01:52:28.336 --> 01:52:33.886
This suggests a decentralized resource-allocation model distinct from the manager pattern.

734
01:52:33.886 --> 01:52:38.373
Strategic Gameplay Under Information Asymmetry: Werewolf.

735
01:52:38.373 --> 01:52:49.461
Werewolf anchors the third dimension of this section, strategic gameplay: under rule constraints and information asymmetry, Agents must reason, deceive, and see through deception.

736
01:52:49.461 --> 01:52:54.636
It provides an architectural counterpoint to the Stanford town that opened this section.

737
01:52:54.636 --> 01:53:08.798
The town allows free interaction in a fully decentralized setting, whereas Werewolf uses a centralized judge + information access control design: a code-driven judge holds the global state and gives each role only the information it should know.

738
01:53:08.798 --> 01:53:15.473
Together, the two cases show how different architectures serve different purposes in Agent-society settings.

739
01:53:15.473 --> 01:53:21.886
Experiment 10-6 advanced difficulty, three stars: : Voice Werewolf Agent System

740
01:53:22.036 --> 01:53:29.086
Werewolf is a classic social-deduction game that tests players' reasoning, deception, and social strategies.

741
01:53:29.036 --> 01:53:35.398
This experiment builds a multi-agent system in which AI Agents play through voice with human players.

742
01:53:35.398 --> 01:53:37.573
Architecture Design:

743
01:53:37.573 --> 01:53:56.223
Game State Management: The Judge (code-driven, not an LLM) maintains a centralized state—player list (one user seat plus AI seats), identities, factions, survival status, game phases (Night/Day/Vote/Resolution), and historical event records.

744
01:53:56.223 --> 01:54:04.261
Information Access Control: The core mechanism of Werewolf is information asymmetry: different roles receive different information.

745
01:54:04.261 --> 01:54:14.311
For example, werewolves know who their teammates are, but villagers do not; the Seer can check one player's identity each night, but only the Seer knows the result.

746
01:54:14.311 --> 01:54:20.348
When the Judge invokes an Agent, it passes only the information available to that Agent's role.

747
01:54:20.348 --> 01:54:22.911
Agent Reasoning and Strategy:

748
01:54:22.911 --> 01:54:27.398
Werewolf Disguise Strategy: "Act like an ordinary villager.

749
01:54:27.398 --> 01:54:33.586
You may voice suspicion about other players, but avoid being so aggressive that you attract attention.

750
01:54:33.586 --> 01:54:40.748
If a player claims to be the Seer and identifies you as a werewolf, counter-accuse them of bluffing as a fake Seer.

751
01:54:40.748 --> 01:54:45.586
When voting, try to follow the majority target to avoid standing out.

752
01:54:45.586 --> 01:54:53.911
Seer Identity Proof: "If several players claim to be the Seer, compare their reported checks with yours and point out contradictions.

753
01:54:53.911 --> 01:55:01.536
If another Seer claimant says they checked a player, watch whether that player's later behavior clearly contradicts the claimed identity.

754
01:55:01.536 --> 01:55:05.311
Ask the Witch to help verify claims when possible.

755
01:55:05.311 --> 01:55:11.048
Villager Logical Reasoning: "Check whether each player's statements are internally consistent.

756
01:55:11.048 --> 01:55:18.098
Pay attention to players who dominate the discussion, remain vague about their role, or repeatedly change position.

757
01:55:18.098 --> 01:55:24.686
Examine voting patterns, because werewolves may coordinate against a non-werewolf player who threatens them.

758
01:55:24.686 --> 01:55:29.773
Base every inference on specific statements or actions rather than speculation.

759
01:55:29.773 --> 01:55:32.086
Acceptance Criteria:

760
01:55:32.086 --> 01:55:45.873
Set up a game with 6-8 players (1 user seat + 5-7 AI Agents); the user seat may be an authorized human or an independent simulator using a real LLM, tools, and a speech round trip

761
01:55:45.873 --> 01:55:54.886
Role configuration: 2 Werewolves, 1 Seer, 1 Witch, the rest are Villagers; the user seat is randomly assigned a role

762
01:55:54.886 --> 01:56:05.461
A simulated user sees only the private/public context authorized for that seat, and its actions must cross a real LLM tool-call → audio → real-ASR boundary

763
01:56:05.461 --> 01:56:11.048
The game can proceed normally for at least 3 complete rounds (Night-Day-Vote cycle)

764
01:56:11.048 --> 01:56:17.186
AI Agents' statements and behaviors are consistent with their role identities and game strategies

765
01:56:17.186 --> 01:56:20.998
Werewolf Agents can effectively hide their identities

766
01:56:20.998 --> 01:56:26.086
Seer Agents can reveal their role and their check results at an appropriate time

767
01:56:26.086 --> 01:56:32.898
Villager Agents' reasoning is based on logical analysis of statements and behaviors, not random guessing

768
01:56:32.898 --> 01:56:36.411
The game can correctly determine the winner at the end

769
01:56:36.411 --> 01:56:41.648
As illustrated in Figure 10-11: Voice Werewolf Agent System.

770
01:56:41.648 --> 01:56:43.536
Chapter Summary.

771
01:56:43.536 --> 01:56:50.023
The value of multi-agent collaboration lies in introducing information unavailable to a single Agent.

772
01:56:50.023 --> 01:57:03.436
Code execution results, visual feedback and verification through external tools can reveal what a single chain of reasoning misses; whether that information gain justifies the additional token cost should be the first design test.

773
01:57:03.436 --> 01:57:10.886
The central design choices are shared or isolated context, and peer, manager or decentralized topology.

774
01:57:10.886 --> 01:57:16.411
Shared context preserves details but can cause context growth and role inertia.

775
01:57:16.411 --> 01:57:28.611
Isolated contexts improve concurrency, modularity and permission control, but require structured handoff packages delivered through tool parameters, shared files or a message bus.

776
01:57:28.611 --> 01:57:38.673
Virtual file systems, Agent lifecycles, message protocols and A2A provide the data plane, control plane and cross-organization interoperability.

777
01:57:38.673 --> 01:57:46.998
Good collaboration exposes interfaces, boundaries, permissions and acceptance criteria—not private chains of thought.

778
01:57:47.164 --> 01:58:02.951
Multi-agent systems can also amplify errors: shared resources create concurrency and semantic conflicts, errors cascade through communication, homogeneous Agents produce common-cause failures, and loops may terminate too early or expand without bound.

779
01:58:02.901 --> 01:58:14.289
Optimistic locking and working-copy isolation, independent cross-validation, diverse information sources, explicit budgets, and cancellation form a basic fault-tolerance loop.

780
01:58:14.289 --> 01:58:23.139
People must not outsource understanding and responsibility together with execution; comprehension debt and cognitive surrender remain real risks.

781
01:58:23.139 --> 01:58:36.039
When short-lived task collaboration grows into long-running, open-ended interaction, social relationships, cultural norms, market competition and strategic behavior under asymmetric information may emerge.

782
01:58:36.039 --> 01:58:42.239
Stronger models or alignment at the individual level do not automatically produce group coordination.

783
01:58:42.239 --> 01:58:53.401
Multi-agent engineering must design how information flows, how capabilities are divided, how incentives are constrained, how disputes are resolved, and how errors are discovered.

784
01:58:53.401 --> 01:58:59.176
Only when these mechanisms are robust can collective intelligence exceed that of an individual.

785
01:58:59.176 --> 01:59:01.114
Thought Questions.

786
01:59:01.276 --> 01:59:11.513
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  In multi-agent collaboration with shared context, subsequent Agents inherit the complete context of preceding Agents.

787
01:59:11.463 --> 01:59:27.826
However, the framing inherited from a previous Agent may bias the judgment of subsequent Agents—for example, a "Code Reviewer" inheriting the context of a "Requirements Analyst" might still approach the task from a requirements perspective rather than a code-quality perspective.

788
01:59:27.826 --> 01:59:31.963
How can this inter-role interference be detected and eliminated?

789
01:59:31.963 --> 01:59:41.138
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  In the manager pattern, the Manager Agent is responsible for task decomposition and result integration.

790
01:59:41.138 --> 01:59:50.876
But the Manager's capabilities limit the performance of the entire system: if it cannot decompose the task correctly, even the strongest sub-agents will be ineffective.

791
01:59:50.876 --> 01:59:55.588
How can the system ensure that the Manager produces a sound decomposition?

792
01:59:55.588 --> 02:00:03.451
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  The decentralized pattern draws on best practices from human organizations.

793
02:00:03.451 --> 02:00:12.263
However, human organizations also have a large number of failure modes—poor communication, buck-passing, goal conflicts.

794
02:00:12.263 --> 02:00:18.376
What "organizational pathologies" do you think are most likely to appear in an Agent society?

795
02:00:18.376 --> 02:00:20.476
How can they be prevented?

796
02:00:20.476 --> 02:00:35.938
advanced difficulty, three stars:  In the manager pattern, when multiple sub-agents execute in parallel, one sub-agent's discovery may render the work of other sub-agents meaningless (e.g., in a search task, one Agent has already found the answer).

797
02:00:35.938 --> 02:00:42.188
Design an efficient cascading termination mechanism to achieve "one succeeds, all stop.

798
02:00:42.188 --> 02:00:51.451
advanced difficulty, three stars:  The optimistic locking mechanism introduced in this chapter resolves concurrent write conflicts for a single file.

799
02:00:51.451 --> 02:01:09.038
However, in a real multi-agent system, shared file systems also face issues such as cross-file semantic conflicts, namespace pollution (Agents creating files arbitrarily, leading to directory chaos), and single points of failure (one Agent mistakenly deleting all files).

800
02:01:09.038 --> 02:01:13.426
How would you design a more robust file system governance mechanism?

801
02:01:13.426 --> 02:01:27.363
advanced difficulty, three stars:  Market-based Agent collaboration (Pinchwork, RentAHuman) introduces transactional relationships: one Agent pays another Agent (or a human) to complete a task.

802
02:01:27.363 --> 02:01:33.051
How can the employer Agent automatically measure the quality of the executor's delivered results?

803
02:01:33.051 --> 02:01:39.851
If the executor claims completion but the employer deems the quality substandard, who arbitrates the dispute?

804
02:01:39.851 --> 02:01:45.238
How can the marketplace prevent low-quality providers from driving out high-quality ones?

805
02:01:45.238 --> 02:01:55.038
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  RentAHuman allows Agents to hire humans via cryptocurrency, reversing the traditional human-machine relationship.

806
02:01:55.038 --> 02:02:00.438
If this model becomes widespread, what role will humans play in the Agent economy?

807
02:02:00.438 --> 02:02:04.863
Will they merely perform physical tasks that Agents cannot complete?

808
02:02:04.863 --> 02:02:17.313
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  Human society needs division of labor because each person's abilities are limited—the frontend developer may not know backend, and the designer may not know ops.

809
02:02:17.313 --> 02:02:21.701
Large models, however, are closer to "generalists.

810
02:02:21.701 --> 02:02:29.226
Research shows that on pure text reasoning tasks, multi-agent debate does not beat a single Agent given equal compute.

811
02:02:29.226 --> 02:02:32.876
So where does the real advantage of multiple Agents lie?

812
02:02:32.876 --> 02:02:43.576
advanced difficulty, three stars:  This chapter treats "shared context" versus "non-shared context" as a core design dimension of multi-agent systems.

813
02:02:43.576 --> 02:02:50.338
Shared context allows all Agents to see the same information, seemingly facilitating coordination.

814
02:02:50.338 --> 02:03:04.638
However, in The Three-Body Problem, the Trisolarans' minds are completely transparent, yet their technological development stagnates; the paperclip thought experiment also shows that when a group converges on the same goal, diversity is lost.

815
02:03:04.638 --> 02:03:09.463
In a multi-agent system, how can we balance efficiency and diversity?

816
02:03:09.628 --> 02:03:16.840
advanced difficulty, three stars:  Assign a Coding Agent a budget of 30 steps and 300 steps.

817
02:03:16.790 --> 02:03:19.403
How should its work strategy differ?

818
02:03:19.403 --> 02:03:29.053
Research shows that simply increasing the step budget does not guarantee performance improvement—Agents may prematurely "saturate" after shallow searches.

819
02:03:29.053 --> 02:03:43.503
Design a "budget-aware" mechanism that allows the Agent to quickly achieve core functionality under a small budget, and to add planning, testing, and review phases under a large budget, fully utilizing the additional computational resources.

820
02:03:43.503 --> 02:03:51.765
intermediate difficulty, two stars:  Table 10-2 maps multi-agent systems onto operating systems row by row.

821
02:03:51.765 --> 02:04:03.003
Extend the table with a few more rows: what do virtual memory and paging, file permissions, deadlock detection, and scheduling algorithms each correspond to in the Agent world?

822
02:04:03.003 --> 02:04:08.465
And which operating-system concepts have no counterpart in the Agent world, and why?

